// Generated by tools/build_appellations.py from the Vitis Mundi atlas. Do not edit by hand.
APX.es = {"country":{"name":"Spain","tagline":"The world's largest vineyard, from Atlantic Albariño to slate-grown Priorat and the flor-veiled bodegas of Jerez.","classification":"Spain's pyramid runs from plain *vino* (no origin beyond Spain) through 43 IGPs, still widely labelled *Vino de la Tierra*, to the protected designations of origin (DOPs). Within the DOPs, the 2003 wine law created several rungs. A *Vino de Calidad con Indicación Geográfica* (VC) is the entry step, meant as a waiting room for a future DO; there were seven in 2026, among them Islas Canarias, Cangas, Lebrija and Cebreros. The *Denominación de Origen* (DO) is the core of the system, with around 70. A *Denominación de Origen Calificada* (DOCa; DOQ in Catalan) needs at least ten years as a DO, higher prices, bottling in the zone and tighter controls: only Rioja (1991) and Priorat (DOQ 2000, national 2009) qualify. A *Vino de Pago* is a single estate with its own DOP, recognised for a distinct microclimate and soil; the winery must make and bottle the wine itself. If the pago lies inside a DOCa it may become a *Vino de Pago Calificado*. The Ministry of Agriculture's list of July 2026 counts 2 DOCa, 70 DO and 27 Vinos de Pago, plus Urbezo, an Aragonese pago registered by the EU in October 2024 but listed under the DO term, so 28 pagos in all.\n\nSpanish labels traditionally talk more about time than place. The national ageing terms are: *joven* (young, little or no oak); *crianza* (red at least 24 months with 6 in oak; white and rosé 18 months with 6 in oak); *reserva* (red 36 months with at least 12 in oak; white and rosé 24 months with 6 in oak); and *gran reserva* (red 60 months with at least 18 in oak; white and rosé 48 months with 6 in oak). Many DOs are stricter: Rioja and Ribera del Duero demand 12 months of barrel for crianza, and Rioja asks 24 for a red gran reserva. *Roble* (oak) is a widely used informal term for a red with a few months in barrel, regulated by some DOs but not by national law. Older terms such as *noble*, *añejo* and *viejo* survive for oxidative and fortified wines. Sherry, Montilla and Málaga have their own systems, built on soleras rather than vintages.\n\nThe recent change is a turn toward the vineyard. Rioja now labels zones, villages (*vino de pueblo*) and *viñedos singulares*; Priorat stacks *vi de vila*, *vi de paratge*, *vinya classificada* and *gran vinya classificada*; Bierzo copies Burgundy with *vino de villa*, *vino de paraje* and classified vineyards; Cava splits into zones and a *Paraje Calificado* summit; Jerez can now print the name of its historic pagos. For drinkers, it means that the name of a village or a slope on a Spanish label is increasingly a promise, not just decoration."},"regions":[{"name":"Rioja","slug":"rioja","tagline":"Spain's most famous red, aged in American and French oak along the Ebro and sheltered by the Sierra de Cantabria.","ids":["es-rioja"]},{"name":"Castilla y León","slug":"castilla-y-le-n","tagline":"The high plateau of the Duero, where Tempranillo and Verdejo ripen under hot days and freezing nights.","ids":["es-abadia-retuerta","es-arlanza","es-arribes","es-cebreros","es-cigales","es-dehesa-penalba","es-leon","es-ribera-del-duero","es-rueda","es-sierra-de-salamanca","es-tierra-del-vino-de-zamora","es-toro","es-uruena","es-valles-de-benavente","es-valtiendas"]},{"name":"Galicia","slug":"galicia","tagline":"Atlantic Spain: granite, rain, Albariño on stone pergolas and Mencía on the river terraces of the Sil.","ids":["es-monterrei","es-ribeira-sacra","es-ribeiro","es-rias-baixas","es-valdeorras"]},{"name":"Basque Country","slug":"basque-country","tagline":"Txakoli: spritzy, salty whites from the green hills above the Bay of Biscay.","ids":["es-arabako-txakolina","es-bizkaiko-txakolina","es-getariako-txakolina"]},{"name":"Asturias","slug":"asturias","tagline":"Steep mountain vineyards of Cangas del Narcea, kept alive by a handful of growers.","ids":["es-cangas"]},{"name":"Navarra","slug":"navarra","tagline":"From Pyrenean foothills to the Ebro's semi-desert: rosado heritage, Garnacha and four single-estate pagos.","ids":["es-bolandin","es-navarra","es-pago-de-arinzano","es-pago-de-otazu","es-prado-de-irache"]},{"name":"Aragón","slug":"arag-n","tagline":"Garnacha's homeland: old bush vines on the slopes of the Moncayo and the Sierra de Algairén, and the cool Somontano below the Pyrenees.","ids":["es-ayles","es-calatayud","es-campo-de-borja","es-carinena","es-somontano","es-urbezo"]},{"name":"Catalonia","slug":"catalonia","tagline":"Cava's homeland and Priorat's black slate: a Mediterranean region of cooperatives, monasteries and restless growers.","ids":["es-alella","es-cataluna","es-conca-de-barbera","es-costers-del-segre","es-emporda","es-montsant","es-pla-de-bages","es-tarragona","es-terra-alta"]},{"name":"Castilla-La Mancha","slug":"castilla-la-mancha","tagline":"Don Quixote's plain: almost half of Spain's vines, Airén and Tempranillo, and more Vinos de Pago than anywhere else.","ids":["es-almansa","es-calzadilla","es-campo-de-calatrava","es-campo-de-la-guardia","es-casa-del-blanco","es-dehesa-del-carrizal","es-dominio-de-valdepusa","es-el-vicario","es-finca-elez","es-guijoso","es-la-jaraba","es-la-mancha","es-los-cerrillos","es-manchuela","es-mondejar","es-mentrida","es-pago-florentino","es-ribera-del-jucar","es-rosalejo","es-rio-negro","es-ucles","es-valdepenas","es-vallegarcia"]},{"name":"Madrid","slug":"madrid","tagline":"The capital's own wine, and the Garnacha revival of the Sierra de Gredos.","ids":["es-vinos-de-madrid"]},{"name":"Valencia","slug":"valencia","tagline":"Bobal on the Requena plateau, Moscatel by the sea and the centuries-old Fondillón of Alicante.","ids":["es-alicante","es-chozas-carrascal","es-el-terrerazo","es-los-balagueses","es-tharsys","es-utiel-requena","es-valencia","es-vera-de-estenas"]},{"name":"Murcia","slug":"murcia","tagline":"Monastrell country: ungrafted bush vines in the near-desert of Jumilla, Yecla and Bullas.","ids":["es-bullas","es-jumilla","es-yecla"]},{"name":"Extremadura","slug":"extremadura","tagline":"Tierra de Barros and the Guadiana: one of Spain's largest vineyards, still little known.","ids":["es-ribera-del-guadiana"]},{"name":"Andalusia","slug":"andalusia","tagline":"Spain's ancient south: flor, solera, raisined Pedro Ximénez and mountain Moscatel.","ids":["es-condado-de-huelva","es-granada","es-lebrija","es-montilla-moriles","es-malaga","es-sierras-de-malaga"]},{"name":"Canary Islands","slug":"canary-islands","tagline":"Volcanic islands with ungrafted pre-phylloxera vines, braided trunks and the ghost of Shakespeare's Canary sack.","ids":["es-abona","es-el-hierro","es-gran-canaria","es-islas-canarias","es-la-gomera","es-la-palma","es-lanzarote","es-tacoronte-acentejo","es-valle-de-guimar","es-valle-de-la-orotava","es-ycoden-daute-isora"]},{"name":"Balearic Islands","slug":"balearic-islands","tagline":"Mallorca's native Manto Negro and Callet, from the Tramuntana foothills to the island's east.","ids":["es-binissalem","es-pla-i-llevant"]},{"name":"Bierzo","slug":"bierzo","tagline":"A mountain bowl in León that behaves like Galicia: Mencía on slate, and Spain's most Burgundian classification.","ids":["es-bierzo"]},{"name":"Penedès","slug":"penedes","tagline":"The heart of Spanish sparkling wine, now the world's first all-organic DO.","ids":["es-cava","es-penedes"]},{"name":"Priorat","slug":"priorat","tagline":"Black slate, Carthusian history and the 1989 revolution that put Spain on the fine-wine map.","ids":["es-priorat"]},{"name":"Sherry Country","slug":"sherry-country","tagline":"The Marco de Jerez: white albariza hills, Atlantic winds and the slow alchemy of flor and solera.","ids":["es-jerez-xeres-sherry","es-manzanilla-sanlucar-de-barrameda"]}],"items":{"es-abadia-retuerta":{"name":"Abadía Retuerta","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla y León","created":"2022","area_ha":193,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Tempranillo (about three-quarters), Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Petit Verdot, Merlot; whites from Albillo Mayor, Sauvignon Blanc, Godello, Verdejo","summary":"A Vino de Pago at Sardón de Duero, just west of the Ribera del Duero boundary, around the 12th-century abbey of Santa María de Retuerta. The estate covers 700 hectares with about 180 hectares of vines. Its wines were long sold as Vino de la Tierra de Castilla y León, and in 2022 it became the 23rd Vino de Pago.","terroir":"Alluvial terraces and slopes along the Duero, with sandy and stony soils.","producers":["Abadía Retuerta"],"nerd_note":"The monastery, founded in 1146, now houses a hotel with a Michelin-starred restaurant in its old refectory.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-abona":{"name":"Abona","cat":"DO","region":"Canary Islands","created":"1990s","area_ha":795,"styles":["white","red","rose","sweet"],"grapes":"Listán Blanco, Malvasía, Albillo Criollo, Vijariego; Listán Negro, Baboso, Tintilla","summary":"Abona, on Tenerife's sunny south, has the highest vineyards in Spain and among the highest in Europe, around Vilaflor at up to about 1,700 m, where old Listán Blanco grows on pumice (*jable*) and volcanic sand. Borja Pérez and Envínate have shown its potential.","terroir":"South-facing slopes from 300 to 1,700 m, sunny and dry, with pumice and sandy volcanic soils.","producers":["Envínate","Bodega Reverón","Bodegas Altos de Trevejos","Cumbres de Abona"],"nerd_note":"Vilaflor de Chasna, at about 1,400 m, is one of Spain's highest villages, with vines planted above it.","rslug":"canary-islands"},"es-alella":{"name":"Alella","cat":"DO","region":"Catalonia","styles":["white","red","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Pansa Blanca (local Xarel·lo), Garnatxa Blanca, Macabeu, Chardonnay; Garnatxa, Ull de Llebre, Cabernet, Merlot","summary":"One of Spain's smallest DOs, squeezed between Barcelona's suburbs and the Maresme coast, Alella has made wine since Roman Laietania. Its granite sand soils (*sauló*) suit Pansa Blanca, the local Xarel·lo. Urban sprawl has shrunk it, but Alta Alella and a few others keep it alive, and Alta Alella's Vallcirera site is a Cava de Paraje Calificado.","terroir":"Granite sand (sauló) slopes facing the sea, warm and humid.","producers":["Alta Alella","Alella Vinícola (Marfil)","Bouquet d'Alella"],"nerd_note":"The Alella Vinícola cooperative building of 1906 is another of Catalonia's modernist wine monuments.","rslug":"catalonia"},"es-alicante":{"name":"Alicante","cat":"DO","region":"Valencia","area_ha":10623,"styles":["red","white","rose","sweet","fortified","sparkling"],"grapes":"Monastrell, Garnacha Tintorera, Tempranillo, Cabernet, Syrah; Moscatel de Alejandría, Merseguera, Macabeo","summary":"Alicante has two subzones: Vinalopó, the inland valleys around Villena, Monòver and Pinoso, where Monastrell grows at 400 to 600 m, and the Marina Alta on the coast near Dénia, home of Moscatel. Its jewel is *Fondillón*, a legendary wine from overripe Monastrell aged at least ten years in oak, often in old soleras, which was once traded across European courts and is said to have been carried on Magellan's circumnavigation. Enrique Mendoza, Gutiérrez de la Vega and Primitivo Quiles keep the traditions.","terroir":"Mediterranean coast and dry inland valleys with limestone and clay soils.","producers":["Enrique Mendoza","Gutiérrez de la Vega","Primitivo Quiles","Bodegas Bocopa","Bernabé Navarro"],"nerd_note":"Fondillón is protected by name in the DO and must be made from Monastrell that is naturally overripe on the vine, without fortification.","contains":["Vinalopó","Marina Alta"],"rslug":"valencia"},"es-almansa":{"name":"Almansa","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"1966","area_ha":10000,"styles":["red","rose","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Garnacha Tintorera (signature), Monastrell, Tempranillo, Syrah; Verdejo, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Almansa, in eastern Albacete on the edge of Valencia and Murcia, is known for Garnacha Tintorera (Alicante Bouschet), a red-fleshed grape that gives very dark wines, often blended with Monastrell. Bodegas Atalaya, of the Gil family, made it known.","terroir":"Plateau at 700 to 1,000 m, with limestone and continental Mediterranean climate.","producers":["Bodegas Atalaya","Bodegas Piqueras","Santa Quiteria"],"nerd_note":"Garnacha Tintorera, one of the few grapes with red flesh, was bred in France in the 1860s.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-arabako-txakolina":{"name":"Arabako Txakolina / Txakolí de Álava / Chacolí de Álava","cat":"DO","region":"Basque Country","created":"2001","area_ha":102,"styles":["white","rose"],"grapes":"Hondarrabi Zuri, Gros Manseng, Petit Manseng, Petit Corbu","summary":"The smallest Txakoli DO, around Amurrio and Ayala in northern Álava, inland on the Atlantic side of the province (far from Rioja Alavesa). About 100 hectares and seven wineries.","terroir":"Inland valleys at 300 to 500 m, cool and wet.","producers":["Astobiza","Bodegas Okendo"],"nerd_note":"It was revived in the 1990s after viticulture had almost vanished from the Aiara valley.","rslug":"basque-country"},"es-arlanza":{"name":"Arlanza","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla y León","created":"2007","area_ha":297,"styles":["red","rose","white"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, with Garnacha, Mencía, Cabernet Sauvignon; Albillo and Viura for whites","summary":"A small, cool DO along the Arlanza river in Burgos and Palencia, around Lerma and Covarrubias, where the monks of San Pedro de Arlanza once made wine. Vineyards sit at 800 to 1,000 m, among the highest in the Duero basin, giving lighter, fresh Tempranillo. Only about 300 hectares and a few dozen growers.","terroir":"High, cold and continental, with limestone and clay soils; frost is a constant risk.","producers":["Sabinares","Buezo","Lerma"],"nerd_note":"The ruined Romanesque monastery of San Pedro de Arlanza, in the valley, is tied to Count Fernán González, the founder of Castile, who was buried there.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-arribes":{"name":"Arribes","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla y León","created":"2007","area_ha":300,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Juan García (local), Rufete, Bruñal, Tempranillo, Mencía; Malvasía (Doña Blanca) for whites","summary":"Arribes follows the Duero's gorge along the Portuguese border in Zamora and Salamanca, across the river from the Douro Superior. Terraced granite and slate slopes drop into a canyon with a surprisingly Mediterranean microclimate, where olives and almonds grow beside old vines of Juan García, a light, peppery red found almost nowhere else. A tiny, beautiful region of about 300 hectares.","terroir":"Steep terraces of granite and slate above the canyon at 400 to 800 m; warm, sheltered microclimate. Fermoselle has a network of medieval cellars under the village.","producers":["La Setera","Ocellum Durii","Almaroja","Bodegas Las Gavias"],"nerd_note":"Bruñal, a rare red grown almost only in Arribes, is genetically identical to Portugal's Alfrocheiro.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-ayles":{"name":"Aylés","cat":"VP","region":"Aragón","created":"2010 (national); EU 2011","area_ha":46,"styles":["red","rose","white"],"grapes":"Garnacha, Tempranillo, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Aragón's first Vino de Pago, the estate of Señorío de Aylés near Mezalocha, south of Zaragoza, around a historic farm on the edge of the Cariñena country. It makes Garnacha-led reds and rosados.","terroir":"Clay, limestone and gravel at about 500 m.","producers":["Pago Aylés"],"nerd_note":"Aylés was registered as a pago in 2010 and by the EU in 2011, the first of two in Aragón.","rslug":"arag-n"},"es-bierzo":{"name":"Bierzo","cat":"DO","region":"Bierzo","created":"1989","area_ha":2276,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Mencía (about 70% of grapes), Garnacha Tintorera, Merenzao, Estaladiña; Godello, Doña Blanca, Palomino","summary":"Bierzo is Spain's Mencía capital, a mountain basin in León where Galicia's Atlantic influence meets the Castilian plateau. Its best wines, from old vineyards on steep slate slopes, have the floral, peppery and mineral freshness that made Mencía fashionable worldwide. The arrival of Álvaro Palacios and his nephew Ricardo Pérez in 1999 (Descendientes de J. Palacios, in Corullón) and the work of native son Raúl Pérez, together with Losada, Dominio de Tares and Castro Ventosa, turned a region of cooperatives into one of the most dynamic in Spain.\n\nBierzo's 2019 classification was a revolution: wines can be Vino de Villa (one village), Vino de Paraje (a named site), Vino de Viña Clasificada and Gran Vino de Viña Clasificada, each with progressively lower yields. The first village and paraje wines reached shops in June 2020. Mencía makes up three-quarters of the old plantings; Godello is the main white.","terroir":"A sheltered basin at 450 to 900 m surrounded by mountains, with clay soils on the valley floor and slate and quartzite on the slopes. About 700 mm of rain, warmer than Galicia's coast.","producers":["Descendientes de J. Palacios","Raúl Pérez","Losada","Dominio de Tares","Castro Ventosa","Pittacum","Bodega del Abad","Verónica Ortega"],"nerd_note":"Bierzo's parajes were defined using the Spanish land registry, and its controls require 100 percent of the grapes from the named place, stricter than the EU's 85 percent rule.","rslug":"bierzo"},"es-binissalem":{"name":"Binissalem","cat":"DO","region":"Balearic Islands","created":"1991","area_ha":580,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Manto Negro (at least 30% in reds), Callet, Cabernet, Syrah, Merlot; Prensal Blanc (Moll), Chardonnay, Moscatel","summary":"Mallorca's first DO, in the centre of the island at the foot of the Serra de Tramuntana, around Binissalem, Santa Maria del Camí and Consell. Manto Negro gives light, fragrant reds. José L. Ferrer, founded in 1931, is the leading house.","terroir":"Calcareous soils with red clay and stones, sheltered by the Tramuntana mountains.","producers":["José L. Ferrer","Macià Batle","Biniagual","Celler Tianna Negre"],"nerd_note":"Manto Negro must make up at least 30 percent of a Binissalem red, a rule that protects the island's native grape.","rslug":"balearic-islands"},"es-bizkaiko-txakolina":{"name":"Bizkaiko Txakolina / Chacolí de Bizkaia / Txakolí de Bizkaia","cat":"DO","region":"Basque Country","created":"1994","area_ha":445,"styles":["white","rose","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Hondarrabi Zuri, Folle Blanche (Munemahatsa), Hondarrabi Beltza, Izkiriota, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, Chardonnay","summary":"The Txakoli of Bizkaia covers vineyards across the province, from Bakio on the coast to the Txorierri valley near Bilbao. It is a little rounder and fuller than Getaria's, with some barrel-fermented and lees-aged wines, and around 35 producers.","terroir":"Wet, cool Atlantic hills with clay and marl.","producers":["Doniene Gorrondona","Itsasmendi","Gorka Izagirre","Bodegas Virgen de Lorea"],"nerd_note":"Bizkaiko producers grow Folle Blanche, locally called Munemahatsa, the same grape once used for Cognac and Armagnac.","rslug":"basque-country"},"es-bolandin":{"name":"Bolandin","cat":"VP","region":"Navarra","created":"2020s","area_ha":114,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Garnacha, Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot","summary":"Navarra's newest pago, a single estate of about 114 hectares registered in the ministry's 2026 list. As with every pago, the winery must grow, make and bottle all the wine on the estate.","terroir":"Southern Navarra, in the Ebro basin.","nerd_note":"Navarra now has four Vinos de Pago, more than any other region except Castilla-La Mancha and Valencia.","rslug":"navarra"},"es-bullas":{"name":"Bullas","cat":"DO","region":"Murcia","created":"1994","area_ha":820,"styles":["red","rose","white"],"grapes":"Monastrell, Tempranillo, Syrah, Garnacha; Macabeo, Airén","summary":"Bullas, in northwest Murcia, grows Monastrell at 400 to 900 m in the mountains of the Noroeste. Higher and cooler than Jumilla, it is a small region with a growing quality scene.","terroir":"Terraced slopes with limestone and stony soils.","producers":["Bodega Balcona","Molino y Lagares","Bodegas del Rosario"],"nerd_note":"Bullas's wine museum is in a 19th-century winery, Bodega Melgares.","rslug":"murcia"},"es-calatayud":{"name":"Calatayud","cat":"DO","region":"Aragón","created":"1990","area_ha":3459,"styles":["red","rose","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Garnacha, Tempranillo, Syrah; Macabeo, Garnacha Blanca, Moscatel","summary":"Calatayud, west of Zaragoza, has some of the highest Garnacha vineyards in Spain (up to 1,000 m) on slate and quartzite slopes along the Jalón and Jiloca. Old bush vines give intense but fresh Garnacha. Producers like El Escocés Volante (Norrel Robertson) and Bodegas Ateca (Juan Gil's group) helped make its name.","terroir":"Mountain slopes at 550 to 1,000 m, continental, with slate, quartzite and gypsum soils.","producers":["El Escocés Volante","Bodegas Ateca","San Alejandro","Bodegas Breca"],"nerd_note":"Calatayud's Garnacha Blanca and Garnacha Tinta often grow together in old field blends, and some vines are ungrafted.","rslug":"arag-n"},"es-calzadilla":{"name":"Calzadilla","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2011","area_ha":13,"styles":["red"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Garnacha, Cabernet, Syrah","summary":"One of Spain's smallest pagos, about 13 hectares at Huete in the Alcarria hills of Cuenca, where limestone and gypsum soils sit at about 900 m. Its reds blend Tempranillo, Garnacha, Cabernet Sauvignon and Syrah grown in the hills, an unexpected landscape in the province better known for the plains of La Mancha.","terroir":"Limestone and gypsum hills at about 900 m.","producers":["Pago Calzadilla"],"nerd_note":"At 13 hectares it is one of the smallest pagos in Spain.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-campo-de-borja":{"name":"Campo de Borja","cat":"DO","region":"Aragón","created":"1980","styles":["red","rose","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Garnacha (dominant), Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah; Macabeo, Moscatel","summary":"Campo de Borja calls itself the Empire of Garnacha, and it has a claim: old vineyards of Garnacha, some more than a century old, on the slopes of the Moncayo at 350 to 700 m. The Borsao group and Alto Moncayo (a project with the Australian winemaker Chris Ringland) built an international following for dense, value Garnacha. The Cistercians of Veruela planted here in the 12th century.","terroir":"Continental and dry, battered by the cold *cierzo* wind, with limestone, clay and stony soils.","producers":["Bodegas Borsao","Alto Moncayo","Bodegas Aragonesas","Bodegas Ruberte"],"nerd_note":"The Cistercian monastery of Veruela, founded in 1146, now houses the DO's wine museum.","rslug":"arag-n"},"es-campo-de-calatrava":{"name":"Campo de Calatrava","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2024","area_ha":15600,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Bobal, Petit Verdot, Garnacha Tintorera; Airén, Macabeo, Verdejo, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Spain's newest DO, registered by the EU in February 2024 as the 102nd Spanish DOP: 16 municipalities in Ciudad Real province, in the area around Almagro and the Ciudad Real plain. It covers a field of extinct volcanoes, the most significant recent volcanic area of the Iberian Peninsula, with soils rich in nickel.","terroir":"Rolling hills at 700 to 900 m and plains at 630 to 650 m, on volcanic and limestone soils.","nerd_note":"The Campo de Calatrava volcanic field has hundreds of small volcanoes and maars, the newest of them only a few thousand years old.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-campo-de-la-guardia":{"name":"Campo de La Guardia","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2009","area_ha":61,"styles":["red"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Petit Verdot, Malbec; Chardonnay","summary":"The pago of Bodegas Martúe in La Guardia, in the province of Toledo, on the plateau between Madrid and the heart of La Mancha. Its stony limestone soils and continental climate give Tempranillo and Bordeaux varieties grown organically on a single estate. Martúe is a member of the Unión de Vinos de Pago.","terroir":"Chalky limestone soils at about 700 m.","producers":["Martúe"],"nerd_note":"Martúe is a member of the Unión de Vinos de Pago.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-cangas":{"name":"Cangas","cat":"VC","region":"Asturias","created":"2008","area_ha":62,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Albarín Negro, Carrasquín, Verdejo Negro, Mencía; Albarín Blanco","summary":"A tiny Vino de Calidad around Cangas del Narcea, in Asturias, with about 60 hectares on steep slate slopes and eight wineries. Its native varieties, Carrasquín and Albarín Negro, give light, peppery reds with high acidity.","terroir":"Slopes at 400 to 900 m above the Narcea, cool and wet.","producers":["Monasterio de Corias","Chacón Buelta"],"nerd_note":"Asturias's only wine designation; the 11th-century Corias monastery nearby is now a Parador.","rslug":"asturias"},"es-carinena":{"name":"Cariñena","cat":"DO","region":"Aragón","created":"1932","area_ha":13531,"styles":["red","rose","white","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Garnacha, Tempranillo, Cariñena (Mazuela), Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot; Macabeo, Garnacha Blanca, Moscatel","summary":"Cariñena is one of Spain's oldest DOs (named in the 1932 statute) and gave its name to the grape the French call Carignan. Paradoxically, the grape is now rare here; Garnacha and Tempranillo dominate. Large cooperatives and family wineries make value reds, and a few producers are bottling old-vine Cariñena and Garnacha, including the renewed *Vino de Calidad de Cariñena* old-vine programme.","terroir":"A plateau and the foothills of the Sierra de Algairén at 400 to 800 m, with slate, limestone and red clay soils and the dry *cierzo* wind.","producers":["Bodegas San Valero","Grandes Vinos","Bodegas Paniza","Bodega Pirineos"],"nerd_note":"Cariñena's name travelled with the grape: Carignan in France, Mazuelo in Rioja, Samsó in Catalonia, Carignano in Sardinia.","rslug":"arag-n"},"es-casa-del-blanco":{"name":"Casa del Blanco","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2010 (EU register 2011)","area_ha":136,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet, Merlot, Syrah, Petit Verdot, Tempranillo; Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"A pago at Manzanares, in the south of the La Mancha plain (Ciudad Real), with alluvial soils on an old river terrace. Its rules allow an unusually wide range of grapes, from local Airén to Cabernet Franc, Malbec, Petit Verdot and Chardonnay, and the estate makes reds and whites under the Pago Casa del Blanco name.","terroir":"Alluvial soils with high magnesium.","producers":["Pago Casa del Blanco"],"nerd_note":"Casa del Blanco is a member of the Unión de Vinos de Pago, the association of single-estate pagos.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-cataluna":{"name":"Cataluña / Catalunya","cat":"DO","region":"Catalonia","created":"1999","area_ha":35467,"styles":["red","white","rose","sweet","sparkling"],"grapes":"Most Catalan varieties plus international grapes","summary":"A regional DO created in 1999 that covers almost every vineyard in Catalonia, letting producers blend across DOs. Large firms like Torres (Sangre de Toro, Viña Sol) use it, and so do small growers working outside their local DO. About 35,500 hectares and 179 wineries.","terroir":"All of Catalonia's climates and soils.","producers":["Familia Torres","Codorníu","Raventós i Blanc"],"nerd_note":"DO Catalunya's creation helps explain the fall of DO Penedès's sales, from about 50 million bottles to 15 million, as firms moved their brands.","rslug":"catalonia"},"es-cava":{"name":"Cava","cat":"DO","region":"Penedès","created":"1986 (EU); Cava name regulated from 1972","area_ha":37502,"styles":["sparkling"],"grapes":"Macabeu (Viura), Xarel·lo, Parellada, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Garnacha, Monastrell, Trepat, Subirat Parent (Malvasía)","summary":"Cava is Spain's traditional-method sparkling wine, made since Josep Raventós's first bottles at Codorníu in 1872, and it is unusual in being defined as much by method as by place: its 157 municipalities are scattered across Catalonia, Aragón, Navarra, La Rioja, the Basque Country, Valencia and Extremadura, though the great majority comes from Catalonia, above all the Penedès. With 37,500 hectares and some 340 producers, it sells around 240 million bottles a year, one of the world's largest sparkling-wine appellations after Champagne and Prosecco.\n\nThe 2020 reform split Cava in two: *Cava de Guarda*, with at least 9 months on lees, and *Cava de Guarda Superior*, which includes Reserva (18 months), Gran Reserva (30 months) and Paraje Calificado (36 months), and from the 2025 harvest must be organic. It also introduced zones and subzones: Comtats de Barcelona (with Valls d'Anoia-Foix, Serra de Mar, Conca del Gaià, Serra de Prades and Pla de Ponent), Valle del Ebro (Alto Ebro and Altiplano de Cariñena), Viñedos de Almendralejo and Levante (Requena). At the top, *Cava de Paraje Calificado* requires a single estate, vines over 10 years old, yields up to 8,000 kg/ha, hand harvesting, estate vinification, vintage dating and 36 months on lees: 15 wines from eight producers in 2026.\n\nDespite these reforms, some of the best growers left. Raventós i Blanc departed in 2012 to create its Conca del Riu Anoia label. Gramona, Recaredo, Llopart, Torelló, Nadal, Sabaté i Coca and others had launched Corpinnat as a collective brand inside Cava, then left the DO in 2019 to sell organic, hand-harvested, estate-made Penedès sparkling wine under that name alone. By 2026 Corpinnat had 21 members selling about three million bottles a year.","terroir":"Mostly the Penedès's limestone and clay soils and Mediterranean climate; also Requena's high plateau and Almendralejo's warm plains.","producers":["Codorníu","Freixenet","Juvé & Camps","Sumarroca","Pere Ventura","Alta Alella","Vins El Cep","Carles Andreu","Castell d'Age","Llopart (now Corpinnat)","Gramona (now Corpinnat)","Recaredo (now Corpinnat)"],"nerd_note":"The Cavas de Paraje Calificado of 2026: Alta Alella's Vallcirera, Carles Andreu's Celdoni, Codorníu's La Fideuera, La Pleta and El Tros Nou, Ferrer Wines' Can Sala, Juvé & Camps's La Capella, Pere Ventura's Can Bas, Sumarroca's Cols, Peretes and Rosendo, and Vins El Cep's Can Prats.","contains":["Comtats de Barcelona","Valle del Ebro","Viñedos de Almendralejo","Levante"],"rslug":"penedes"},"es-cebreros":{"name":"Cebreros","cat":"VC","region":"Castilla y León","created":"2017 (EU 2019)","area_ha":500,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Garnacha, Albillo Real","summary":"Cebreros, in the province of Ávila on the northern slopes of the Sierra de Gredos, is part of the Gredos Garnacha revival. Old bush vines of Garnacha at 700 to 1,100 m on granite and slate give fine, perfumed reds, and Albillo Real fresh whites. Producers like Rafael Mancebo, Daniel Landi and the Comando G team helped bring attention.","terroir":"Granite and slate slopes, with a continental mountain climate.","producers":["Bodega Garnacha Alto Alberche","Daniel Landi","Rafael Mancebo"],"nerd_note":"Before the Gredos revival, Cebreros supplied bulk Garnacha to Madrid's taverns.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-chozas-carrascal":{"name":"Chozas Carrascal","cat":"VP","region":"Valencia","created":"2020 (EU)","area_ha":31,"styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Bobal, Tempranillo, Garnacha, Cabernet, Syrah, Monastrell; Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Macabeo","summary":"The López-Peidro family estate at San Antonio de Requena, farmed organically on the Utiel-Requena plateau at about 700 m. Its rules cover a long list of grapes, from Bobal and Monastrell to Cabernet Franc and Chardonnay, and the estate makes still wines and traditional-method sparkling wines under the pago name.","terroir":"Limestone and clay at about 700 m.","producers":["Chozas Carrascal"],"nerd_note":"It makes both still and sparkling wines under the same pago name.","rslug":"valencia"},"es-cigales":{"name":"Cigales","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla y León","created":"1991","area_ha":1904,"styles":["rose","red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Garnacha, with white Verdejo, Albillo and Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Cigales, just north of Valladolid along the Pisuerga, was long the city's source of claret: rosados and light reds made from a mix of red and white grapes, aged in hillside cellars. The DO keeps that tradition with its *rosado* and the old field blend style, but most wine now is red Tempranillo. About 1,900 hectares, many old bush vines, and a growing number of characterful growers.","terroir":"Terraces and hills of the Pisuerga at 700 to 800 m, with sandy and stony soils over limestone, and a cold continental climate.","producers":["César Príncipe","Bodega Finca Museum","Traslanzas","Bodegas Hijos de Félix Salas"],"nerd_note":"Cigales still makes its traditional rosado by blending white grapes with red, the old claret recipe; the old cellars of Mucientes and Cubillas de Santa Marta are dug into the hillsides.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-conca-de-barbera":{"name":"Conca de Barberà","cat":"DO","region":"Catalonia","area_ha":2656,"styles":["rose","red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Trepat (native), Garnatxa, Ull de Llebre, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot; Macabeu, Parellada, Chardonnay","summary":"A basin in inland Tarragona around Montblanc and the Cistercian monastery of Poblet, Conca de Barberà is home to Trepat, a light, spicy red grape used for rosado and pink Cava and now for elegant reds. It also supplies Parellada to Cava, and is the site of Torres's Milmanda Chardonnay and Grans Muralles from the castle walls at Poblet.","terroir":"A sheltered valley at 350 to 600 m, cooler than the coast, with limestone soils.","producers":["Torres (Grans Muralles, Milmanda)","Carles Andreu","Celler Escoda-Sanahuja","Abadia de Poblet"],"nerd_note":"Poblet, a UNESCO World Heritage Site and royal pantheon of the kings of Aragón, still has monks and still makes wine.","rslug":"catalonia"},"es-condado-de-huelva":{"name":"Condado de Huelva","cat":"DO","region":"Andalusia","created":"1960s","area_ha":2028,"styles":["white","fortified","sweet"],"grapes":"Zalema (local), Palomino, Listán de Huelva, Garrido Fino, Moscatel, Pedro Ximénez","summary":"Condado de Huelva, between Seville and the Portuguese border near the Doñana marshes, has made wine since Roman times and has a Columbus legend: its wines are said to have sailed on his voyages from nearby Palos de la Frontera and Moguer. It makes young whites from the local Zalema and Sherry-style wines: *Condado Pálido* (biologically aged) and *Condado Viejo* (oxidative), plus sweet wines.","terroir":"Flat land of clay, loam and sand near the Atlantic and the Doñana marshes; hot summers moderated by sea air.","producers":["Bodegas Díaz","Bodegas Iglesias","Oliveros","Bodegas Sauci"],"nerd_note":"Zalema, the local white, is almost unknown outside Huelva.","rslug":"andalusia"},"es-costers-del-segre":{"name":"Costers del Segre","cat":"DO","region":"Catalonia","created":"1988","area_ha":3940,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Garnatxa, Syrah; Macabeu, Xarel·lo, Parellada, Chardonnay","summary":"An interior DO in the province of Lleida with seven subzones (Artesa, Garrigues, Pallars Jussà, Raimat, Segrià, Valls del Riu Corb and Urgell), from dry plains to pre-Pyrenean slopes. Its story begins at Raimat, where Manuel Raventós of Codorníu bought desert land in 1914, irrigated it from the Urgell canal and planted vines and pines. Castell del Remei is the other historic name.","terroir":"Continental, dry (300 to 400 mm of rain) and cold-wintered, on limestone and gravel; irrigation is allowed and common.","producers":["Raimat","Castell del Remei","Tomàs Cusiné","Clos Pons","Castell d'Encús"],"nerd_note":"Raimat's 1918 winery, by Gaudí's disciple Joan Rubió i Bellver, was among the first in Spain built in reinforced concrete.","contains":["Artesa","Garrigues","Pallars Jussà","Raimat","Segrià","Valls del Riu Corb","Urgell"],"rslug":"catalonia"},"es-dehesa-del-carrizal":{"name":"Dehesa del Carrizal","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2006 (EU register 2009)","area_ha":26,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot, Tempranillo; Chardonnay","summary":"A pago at Retuerta del Bullaque, in the Montes de Toledo (Ciudad Real), on a former hunting estate at about 900 m between oak woodland and the Cabañeros National Park. Planted with Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot, Petit Verdot, Tempranillo and Chardonnay, it shows how the mountains on the northern edge of La Mancha give fresher wines than the plain.","terroir":"Slate and clay hills in the Montes de Toledo.","producers":["Dehesa del Carrizal"],"nerd_note":"It shares its municipality with Vallegarcía, another pago.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-dehesa-penalba":{"name":"Dehesa Peñalba","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla y León","created":"2022","area_ha":78,"styles":["red"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot","summary":"A Vino de Pago in the municipality of Villabáñez (Valladolid), on the south bank of the Duero west of Ribera, named after Peñalba de Duero, a long-abandoned village. Approved by the EU in February 2022 for red wines only, from Tempranillo, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot on warm, sandy, gravelly soils.","terroir":"Warm, sandy, gravelly, well-drained soils.","producers":["Bodegas Vizar"],"nerd_note":"One of three single-estate pagos in Castilla y León, all approved between 2021 and 2022.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-dominio-de-valdepusa":{"name":"Dominio de Valdepusa","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2003","area_ha":49,"styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Petit Verdot, Graciano","summary":"Spain's first Vino de Pago, the estate of Carlos Falcó, Marqués de Griñón, at Malpica de Tajo in Toledo. Falcó studied at UC Davis, planted Cabernet in 1974 and brought in Richard Smart's canopy-management techniques and drip irrigation. His Syrah and Petit Verdot proved that the La Mancha fringes could make fine wine. Falcó died in 2020; the estate is now run by his children.","terroir":"Clay over limestone in the Tagus valley, at about 450 m.","producers":["Marqués de Griñón"],"nerd_note":"Falcó led the campaign for the Vino de Pago category and the Grandeza de España association of single-estate producers.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-el-hierro":{"name":"El Hierro","cat":"DO","region":"Canary Islands","created":"1990s","area_ha":121,"styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Vijariego, Listán Blanco, Verdello; Baboso, Listán Negro, Vijariego Negro","summary":"The westernmost and smallest DO of the Canaries, on the island of El Hierro, with vineyards around the El Golfo valley (Frontera). Baboso Negro, a dark and spicy red, and Vijariego are its signatures.","terroir":"Volcanic slopes from sea level to 700 m.","producers":["Bodega Uwe Urbach","Cooperativa del Campo de Frontera"],"nerd_note":"El Hierro was once the reference meridian for world maps, the edge of the Old World.","rslug":"canary-islands"},"es-el-terrerazo":{"name":"El Terrerazo","cat":"VP","region":"Valencia","area_ha":63,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Bobal, Tempranillo, Garnacha, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah","summary":"Toni Sarrión's Mustiguillo estate near Utiel, the first Vino de Pago in the Valencian Community, and the place where Bobal was first proven as a fine wine (Mestizaje, Finca Terrerazo, Quincha Corral).","terroir":"Limestone and clay at about 800 m.","producers":["Mustiguillo"],"nerd_note":"Sarrión also rescued Merseguera for his white Mestizaje Blanco.","rslug":"valencia"},"es-el-vicario":{"name":"El Vicario","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2019","area_ha":121,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Petit Verdot, Graciano, Garnacha; Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"The pago of Pago del Vicario in the municipality of Ciudad Real, registered by the EU in 2019. The estate grows a wide range of reds and two whites on clay and gravel near the Guadiana and makes red, white and rosé, one of the few single-estate DOPs on the open plain around the provincial capital.","terroir":"Clay and gravel at about 650 m.","producers":["Pago del Vicario"],"nerd_note":"Pago del Vicario is a member of the Unión de Vinos de Pago.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-emporda":{"name":"Empordà","cat":"DO","region":"Catalonia","created":"1975","area_ha":1818,"styles":["red","white","rose","sweet","fortified"],"grapes":"Garnatxa, Samsó (Carinyena), Cabernet, Merlot, Syrah; Garnatxa Blanca, Garnatxa Roja (Grisa), Macabeu, Moscatell","summary":"Empordà, in the far northeast against the French border and the Costa Brava, was where the Greeks of Empúries landed in the 6th century BC. The tramuntana wind, which can blow for days, keeps vines healthy. Its best wines include old-vine Garnatxa and Carinyena reds and the sweet, rancio-like *Garnatxa de l'Empordà*, aged in barrels and glass demijohns in the sun.","terroir":"From the Albera foothills to the Cap de Creus terraces; granite and slate in the north, alluvium and clay in the south.","producers":["Perelada","Martí Fabra","Celler Espelt","Masia Serra","Vinyes dels Aspres","Terra Remota"],"nerd_note":"Phylloxera reached Spain through Empordà in 1879, crossing from France; the terraces of Cap de Creus were abandoned and are now being replanted.","rslug":"catalonia"},"es-finca-elez":{"name":"Finca Élez","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2002 (regional); EU register 2009","area_ha":39,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Tempranillo; Chardonnay","summary":"The estate of the film producer Manuel Manzaneque at El Bonillo in Albacete, at about 1,000 m. One of the highest vineyards in Castilla-La Mancha, it became famous for a barrel-fermented Chardonnay.","terroir":"Limestone plateau at 1,000 m with cold nights.","producers":["Manuel Manzaneque"],"nerd_note":"With Guijoso, it shares the El Bonillo municipality.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-getariako-txakolina":{"name":"Getariako Txakolina / Chacolí de Getaria / Txakolí de Getaria","cat":"DO","region":"Basque Country","created":"1989","area_ha":458,"styles":["white","rose","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Hondarrabi Zuri, Hondarrabi Beltza, Gros Manseng, Petit Manseng, Petit Corbu","summary":"The most famous Txakoli, from the fishing town of Getaria and the Gipuzkoa coast near San Sebastián. Its wines are bright, lightly spritzy, saline and low in alcohol (often 10.5 to 11.5 percent), the classic partner to anchovies, grilled fish and pintxos, poured from a height to raise the fizz. The DO was created in 1989 with a few dozen hectares and covers about 460 today.","terroir":"Steep slopes of clay and sandstone facing the Bay of Biscay; very wet (1,600 mm), mild, windy.","producers":["Txomin Etxaniz","Ameztoi","Gaintza","Talai Berri","Akarregi Txiki"],"nerd_note":"Ameztoi's Rubentis, a pink Txakoli, became a cult wine in New York bars in the 2010s.","rslug":"basque-country"},"es-gran-canaria":{"name":"Gran Canaria","cat":"DO","region":"Canary Islands","created":"2005","area_ha":191,"styles":["red","white","rose","sweet"],"grapes":"Listán Negro, Negramoll, Tintilla, Vijariego Negro; Listán Blanco, Malvasía, Moscatel","summary":"The DO merged the island's earlier Monte Lentiscal DO in 2005. Its historic vineyards are around the Caldera de Bandama, a volcanic crater near Las Palmas, where the 'vino del Monte' was famous.","terroir":"Volcanic soils from sea level to 1,500 m.","producers":["Bodegas Los Berrazales","Bodega San Juan del Mocanal","Bodega Mondalón"],"nerd_note":"Bandama's crater has a farm with vines on its floor, 200 m below the rim.","rslug":"canary-islands"},"es-granada":{"name":"Granada","cat":"DO","region":"Andalusia","created":"2009 (as VC); now DO","area_ha":254,"styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Garnacha, Syrah, Cabernet, Merlot, Pinot Noir; Vijiriego, Moscatel, Pedro Ximénez, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Granada covers vineyards at some of Spain's highest altitudes, up to about 1,300 m in the Alpujarra and Contraviesa on the southern slopes of the Sierra Nevada. Local *vino de la costa* was a rosado-like field blend; today a few producers make fresh whites from Vijiriego and Moscatel and reds from Tempranillo and Syrah.","terroir":"High slopes of slate and schist with Mediterranean influence from the sea and mountain coolness.","producers":["Bodega Barranco Oscuro","Bodegas Fontedei","Cortijo El Cura"],"nerd_note":"Barranco Oscuro in Cádiar claims some of the highest vineyards in Europe, at about 1,300 m.","rslug":"andalusia"},"es-guijoso":{"name":"Guijoso","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2004 (EU register 2007)","area_ha":118,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Tempranillo; Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"The pago of the Sánchez Muliterno family at El Bonillo, in the province of Albacete, on a high limestone plateau at about 1,000 m. It was one of the first single-estate pagos after Dominio de Valdepusa. The altitude gives cold nights and slow ripening, so its Chardonnay and Bordeaux-style reds keep a freshness unusual for La Mancha.","terroir":"Stony limestone at about 1,000 m.","producers":["Sánchez Muliterno"],"nerd_note":"Guijoso and Finca Élez, two pagos in one municipality, show how high (about 1,000 m) the cool fringes of La Mancha reach.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-islas-canarias":{"name":"Islas Canarias","cat":"VC","region":"Canary Islands","created":"2011","area_ha":503,"styles":["white","red","rose","sweet","sparkling"],"grapes":"All Canary varieties","summary":"A regional designation created in 2011 covering all the islands, allowing blends across islands and DOs. It is used by producers who source from several islands or make wines outside the rules of their island's DO. The ministry lists it as a Vino de Calidad.","terroir":"All Canary volcanic terroirs.","producers":["Suertes del Marqués","Envínate","Bodegas Monje"],"nerd_note":"Several of the islands' most famous modern wines are labelled Islas Canarias rather than their local DO.","rslug":"canary-islands"},"es-jerez-xeres-sherry":{"name":"Jerez-Xérès-Sherry / Jerez / Xérès / Sherry","cat":"DO","region":"Sherry Country","created":"1932 (Estatuto); Consejo 1935","area_ha":7027,"styles":["fortified","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Palomino Fino, Pedro Ximénez, Moscatel de Alejandría; historic Beba, Perruno, Vigiriega and others since 2021","summary":"Sherry is Spain's greatest gift to wine and one of the most misunderstood wines in the world. From a neutral grape (Palomino) grown on white albariza soils near the Atlantic, the bodegas of Jerez, El Puerto and Sanlúcar make a whole family of styles through two kinds of ageing: biological, under a veil of yeast (flor) that keeps the wine pale, dry and sharp, and oxidative, in contact with air, which makes it amber, nutty and rich. A fino is one of the driest, most savoury wines on earth; a 30-year-old palo cortado or oloroso one of the most complex; a PX, made from raisined grapes, so sweet and dense it can be poured over ice cream. And all are made in soleras, fractional blending systems in which wines decades old feed the young.\n\nThe production zone covers ten municipalities in Cádiz and Seville, with about 7,000 hectares of vineyard (7,027 in 2022). In 2023/24 Jerez marketed almost 18 million litres. Its main markets are Spain, the UK, the Netherlands, Germany and the US. Sales today are a fraction of their 1970s peak, but quality has never been higher, helped by *en rama* bottlings, rare almacenista and single-cask wines, VOS and VORS certified old wines, and a new generation of growers bottling single pagos.\n\nThe 2021 reform, the largest since the DO began, let wines age anywhere in the zone (in force from October 2022), allowed pago names on labels (85 percent rule), brought back historic grape varieties, created Fino Viejo and Manzanilla Pasada categories for wines over seven years old, and opened the door to unfortified Sherry, wines that reach the 15 percent needed for flor naturally, aged at least two years. Some of these major changes needed EU approval and came into use in stages.","terroir":"Albariza (chalky marl with diatom and radiolarian fossils) dominates the best pagos north and west of Jerez: Macharnudo, Carrascal, Balbaína, Añina, Los Tercios. It retains winter rain and forms a crust in summer. The climate is hot (summer highs often above 35 degrees) but tempered by the humid Atlantic poniente, with about 600 mm of rain falling mostly in winter. The hot dry levante wind is the enemy of flor.","producers":["González Byass","Lustau","Valdespino","Fernando de Castilla","Bodegas Tradición","Emilio Hidalgo","Williams & Humbert","Osborne","Gutiérrez Colosía","Sandeman","Harveys","Equipo Navazos","El Maestro Sierra","Bodegas Grant"],"nerd_note":"Valdespino's Inocente is one of the very few finos still fermented in barrel, from a single pago (Macharnudo Alto) and aged through ten criaderas.","contains":["Macharnudo","Carrascal","Balbaína","Añina","Los Tercios","Miraflores"],"rslug":"sherry-country"},"es-jumilla":{"name":"Jumilla","cat":"DO","region":"Murcia","created":"1966","area_ha":21447,"styles":["red","rose","white","sweet","fortified"],"grapes":"Monastrell (dominant), Garnacha Tintorera, Syrah, Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Petit Verdot; Airén, Macabeo, Malvasía","summary":"Jumilla, straddling Murcia and Albacete, is the heartland of Monastrell. Its sandy, chalky soils kept phylloxera out until 1989, when the louse finally arrived and devastated the old ungrafted vineyards; replanting came with a modern turn. Today producers like Casa Castillo, whose José María Vicente makes some of Spain's most elegant Monastrell, Juan Gil, Bodegas Luzón and the cooperatives make wines that range from rich to fresh and stony.","terroir":"High plateaus at 400 to 900 m, very dry (about 300 mm), with sandy, chalky limestone soils that drain freely.","producers":["Casa Castillo","Juan Gil","Bodegas Luzón","Bodegas Carchelo","Bodegas Bleda"],"nerd_note":"Casa Castillo's Pie Franco comes from a parcel of ungrafted Monastrell planted in 1941 that survived the phylloxera of 1989 on sand.","rslug":"murcia"},"es-la-gomera":{"name":"La Gomera","cat":"DO","region":"Canary Islands","created":"2000s","area_ha":125,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Forastera Blanca (Gomera), Listán Blanco, Marmajuelo; Listán Negro, Negramoll","summary":"A small DO on the steep, ravine-cut island of La Gomera, where Forastera Blanca, a local white, grows on tiny terraces. About 125 hectares and 20 wineries.","terroir":"Steep ravines and terraces from 300 to 1,000 m.","producers":["Bodega Insular de La Gomera"],"nerd_note":"La Gomera's terraced ravines were once cultivated almost to the summit.","rslug":"canary-islands"},"es-la-jaraba":{"name":"La Jaraba","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2019","area_ha":75,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Graciano; Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"The pago of the Pago de la Jaraba family winery at El Provencio, in the province of Cuenca on the eastern La Mancha plain. Registered by the EU in 2019, it makes reds from Tempranillo, Cabernet, Merlot and Graciano and a Sauvignon Blanc, from limestone and clay at about 700 m.","terroir":"Limestone and clay at about 700 m.","producers":["Pago de la Jaraba"],"nerd_note":"La Jaraba is a member of the Unión de Vinos de Pago.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-la-mancha":{"name":"La Mancha","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","area_ha":146911,"styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Airén, Tempranillo (Cencibel), Garnacha, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot, Petit Verdot; Macabeo, Verdejo, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay","summary":"La Mancha is the largest DO in Spain and one of the largest delimited wine regions in the world, with about 147,000 registered hectares across 190 municipalities in Ciudad Real, Cuenca, Toledo and Albacete. Only a fraction of the vineyard's harvest is bottled under the DO; most goes to bulk wine, brandy and grape concentrate. Its historic grape is Airén, a hardy white that gives neutral wine, but the region is now dominated by Tempranillo and international varieties. Quality has risen sharply with modern cooperatives and estates, and the DO makes reliable, low-priced reds and whites.","terroir":"A huge, flat plateau at 600 to 750 m with hot summers, cold winters and about 300 to 400 mm of rain; red clay and limestone soils.","producers":["Bodegas Ayuso","Vinícola de Tomelloso","Bodegas Verum","Bodegas Volver","Finca Antigua"],"nerd_note":"Tomelloso, the region's wine and brandy town, sits on thousands of underground *cuevas* dug to keep wine cool in huge earthenware *tinajas*.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-la-palma":{"name":"La Palma","cat":"DO","region":"Canary Islands","created":"1990s","area_ha":435,"styles":["white","red","sweet","rose"],"grapes":"Malvasía Aromática, Listán Blanco, Albillo Criollo, Bujariego (Vijariego); Negramoll, Listán Negro","summary":"La Palma has three subzones: Fuencaliente in the volcanic south, home of sweet Malvasía; Hoyo de Mazo in the east; and the cool north, where *vino de tea* is aged in barrels of Canary pine (*tea*), which gives a resinous flavour. The 2021 Cumbre Vieja eruption damaged vineyards and wineries on the west of the island.","terroir":"Volcanic soils from fresh ash to old weathered clays; altitudes up to 1,500 m.","producers":["Bodegas Teneguía","Victoria Torres (Matías i Torres)","Bodegas Carballo","Juan Matías Torres"],"nerd_note":"*Vino de tea* is a living relic of the days when the islands had no oak, so barrels were made of resinous Canary pine heartwood.","contains":["Fuencaliente","Hoyo de Mazo","Norte de La Palma"],"rslug":"canary-islands"},"es-lanzarote":{"name":"Lanzarote","cat":"DO","region":"Canary Islands","created":"1993","area_ha":1895,"styles":["white","sweet","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Malvasía Volcánica (dominant), Listán Blanco, Diego (Vijariego), Moscatel; Listán Negro, Negramoll","summary":"Lanzarote's vineyards are a work of land art. After the Timanfaya eruptions of 1730 to 1736 buried the island's farms under black volcanic ash (*picón*), farmers dug funnel-shaped pits (*hoyos*), some several metres deep, down to the old soil, planted a vine at the bottom and built a low semicircular stone wall (*zoco*) on the windward side. The ash acts as a mulch, trapping night dew in a place with almost no rain. La Geria, the most spectacular area, is a protected landscape of thousands of hoyos.\n\nMalvasía Volcánica, a local crossing of Malvasía and Marmajuelo, gives fresh, saline dry whites and the traditional sweet Malvasía. Bodegas El Grifo, founded in 1775, is one of the oldest wineries in the Canaries; Los Bermejos, Stratvs and Puro Rofe lead the modern scene.","terroir":"Black volcanic ash (picón) over older red soil, near-zero rainfall (about 150 mm), strong trade winds.","producers":["El Grifo","Los Bermejos","Stratvs","Bodega La Geria","Puro Rofe","Vega de Yuco"],"nerd_note":"Each hoyo usually holds just one to three vines, giving La Geria one of the lowest planting densities of any vineyard in the world.","rslug":"canary-islands"},"es-lebrija":{"name":"Lebrija","cat":"VC","region":"Andalusia","created":"2009","area_ha":19,"styles":["white","fortified","sweet"],"grapes":"Palomino, Moscatel, Vijiriega, Sauvignon Blanc; Tintilla de Rota","summary":"A tiny Vino de Calidad in the Seville town of Lebrija, which also belongs to the Jerez production zone. It covers wines of the town not made as Sherry: still whites, biologically aged *Lebrija viejo*, and sweet wines. Only one winery was active in 2023/24.","terroir":"Albariza and clay on the edge of the Guadalquivir marshes.","producers":["Bodegas González Palacios"],"nerd_note":"Lebrija's tradition of 'vino de la tierra' predates the Sherry ageing rules that kept it outside the Jerez name.","rslug":"andalusia"},"es-leon":{"name":"León","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla y León","created":"2007","area_ha":1216,"styles":["red","rose","white"],"grapes":"Prieto Picudo, Mencía; Albarín Blanco, Verdejo, Godello","summary":"The DO León (originally Tierra de León) covers the plains south of the city, around Valencia de Don Juan and Valdevimbre, and is the home of Prieto Picudo, a small-berried, fresh red, and Albarín Blanco. Prieto Picudo traditionally made *rosado de aguja*, lightly fizzy, but today gives dark, savoury reds. About 1,200 hectares.","terroir":"Plateau at 750 to 900 m, cold winters, alluvial terraces with stones and clay.","producers":["Bodegas Margón","Leyenda del Páramo","Gordonzello"],"nerd_note":"In Valdevimbre, growers still dig *cuevas*, underground cellars where rosado was traditionally made with the *madreo* method, adding whole bunches to the fermenting wine.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-los-balagueses":{"name":"Los Balagueses","cat":"VP","region":"Valencia","created":"2011","area_ha":18,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Merlot, Tempranillo; Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"A pago of Bodegas Vegalfaro in the municipality of Requena, on the Utiel-Requena plateau at about 750 m. Unusually for the area it does not include Bobal: its rules allow Syrah, Merlot, Tempranillo, Chardonnay and Sauvignon Blanc. At about 18 hectares it is one of the smallest pagos, shared by two registered wineries in 2023/24.","terroir":"Plateau at about 750 m.","producers":["Vegalfaro"],"nerd_note":"Vegalfaro is a member of the Unión de Vinos de Pago.","rslug":"valencia"},"es-los-cerrillos":{"name":"Los Cerrillos","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2019","area_ha":34,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Petit Verdot; Chardonnay","summary":"The pago of Bodegas Montalvo Wilmot at Argamasilla de Alba, in Ciudad Real, registered by the EU in 2019. The town is traditionally identified as the 'place in La Mancha' whose name Cervantes did not want to remember, and the estate makes Tempranillo-led reds and Chardonnay on limestone and red clay.","terroir":"Limestone and red clay on the La Mancha plain.","producers":["Montalvo Wilmot"],"nerd_note":"Argamasilla de Alba is traditionally identified as the 'village in La Mancha' whose name Cervantes did not want to remember.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-malaga":{"name":"Málaga","cat":"DO","region":"Andalusia","created":"1933","area_ha":440,"styles":["sweet","fortified","white"],"grapes":"Moscatel de Alejandría, Moscatel Morisco (Moscatel de grano menudo), Pedro Ximénez","summary":"Málaga was one of Europe's most famous wines from the 17th to the 19th century, a sweet, dark wine known in England as 'Mountain' and loved from London to St Petersburg. In 1878 phylloxera was found near Málaga, the first case in Spain, and the vineyards never fully recovered. The DO covers sweet and fortified wines: *Málaga Dulce*, made from sun-dried Moscatel or PX, with or without fortification and aged for years (Pálido, Noble, Añejo, Trasañejo), *Lágrima* (free-run must), and naturally sweet *vino dulce natural*. In 2001 a sister DO, Sierras de Málaga, was created for dry wines, and the two share a Consejo Regulador.","terroir":"Two zones: the Axarquía, east of the city, on steep slate slopes between the sea and the mountains, with Moscatel; and the Montes de Málaga, north of the city. Grapes are dried on *paseras* in the sun.","producers":["Antigua Casa de Guardia","Bodegas Málaga Virgen","Bodegas Quitapenas","Bodegas Dimobe","Compañía de Vinos Telmo Rodríguez","Jorge Ordóñez & Co.","Bodegas Bentomiz"],"nerd_note":"The Axarquía's raisin-drying paseras are recognised by the FAO as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System.","contains":["Axarquía","Montes de Málaga"],"rslug":"andalusia"},"es-manchuela":{"name":"Manchuela","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2000","area_ha":10096,"styles":["red","rose","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Bobal, Tempranillo, Garnacha, Monastrell, Syrah, Garnacha Tintorera; Macabeo, Albillo, Moscatel","summary":"Between La Mancha and the Valencian border, Manchuela lies on a plateau at 600 to 1,000 m along the Júcar and Cabriel rivers. Bobal is its strength, and the DO's revival owes a lot to Finca Sandoval, founded by the wine writer Víctor de la Serna, and to Juan Antonio Ponce's natural Bobals.","terroir":"Limestone and clay plateau, continental with Mediterranean influence from the east.","producers":["Finca Sandoval","Bodegas Ponce","Bodegas Altolandón","Cooperativa San Antonio Abad"],"nerd_note":"Juan Antonio Ponce, who trained with Telmo Rodríguez, ferments old-vine Bobal with whole bunches like a Beaujolais grower.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-manzanilla-sanlucar-de-barrameda":{"name":"Manzanilla-Sanlúcar de Barrameda / Manzanilla","cat":"DO","region":"Sherry Country","created":"1964 (separate DO)","area_ha":7027,"styles":["fortified"],"grapes":"Palomino Fino (only)","summary":"Manzanilla is fino's coastal twin, a biologically aged dry wine that must be aged in the bodegas of Sanlúcar de Barrameda, where the Guadalquivir meets the Atlantic opposite the Doñana marshes. The humidity there keeps the flor thick and alive all year, so manzanilla is lighter, fresher and saltier than fino, with notes of chamomile (*manzanilla* in Spanish, though the name may come from the village of Manzanilla in Huelva), green apple, bread dough and sea air. It is the most-drunk Sherry style in Spain, served chilled with prawns, fried fish and jamón.\n\nThe DO shares its vineyard and its Consejo Regulador with Jerez. In 2023/24 it marketed about 6 million litres. Its houses are some of the oldest in the region: Delgado Zuleta (1744), Hidalgo La Gitana (1792), Barbadillo (1821). *Manzanilla Pasada*, an older manzanilla with a slightly richer, nuttier edge, now must have at least seven years. Since the 2020s the Consejo has also proposed an unfortified manzanilla, as climate change now often brings Palomino to 15 percent naturally.","terroir":"The Sanlúcar side of the Marco has some of its best albariza pagos, Miraflores, Maína, Balbaína, Callejuela, Martín Miguel and Pastrana, close to the sea. The town's bodegas, especially in the lower town near the river, are humid and cool, ideal for flor.","producers":["Barbadillo","Hidalgo La Gitana","Delgado Zuleta","Bodegas Argüeso","La Guita (Hijos de Rainera Pérez Marín)","Cota 45 (Ramiro Ibáñez)","Bodegas Callejuela","Equipo Navazos"],"nerd_note":"Bodegas in Sanlúcar's lower town (*barrio bajo*) near the river give a different manzanilla from those in the upper town (*barrio alto*): the closer to the river, the thicker the flor.","contains":["Miraflores","Maína","Callejuela","Martín Miguel","Pastrana","Balbaína"],"rslug":"sherry-country"},"es-mentrida":{"name":"Méntrida","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"1976","area_ha":5800,"styles":["red","rose","white"],"grapes":"Garnacha, Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah; Albillo Real, Macabeo","summary":"Méntrida, in northern Toledo where the plains meet the Sierra de Gredos, was long a bulk Garnacha supplier for Madrid. Since the 2000s the Jiménez-Landi family and others have shown its old Garnacha on granite can be fine and perfumed.","terroir":"Sandy granite soils (from Gredos) and clay, at 400 to 800 m.","producers":["Jiménez-Landi","Bodegas Arrayán","Canopy","Bodegas Tierra Calma"],"nerd_note":"Méntrida's best vineyards rise toward the Sierra de Gredos in villages like Hormigos and El Real de San Vicente.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-mondejar":{"name":"Mondéjar","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"1996","area_ha":358,"styles":["white","red","rose"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Garnacha; Malvar, Macabeo, Torrontés","summary":"A small DO in southwest Guadalajara, near the Tagus, with about 360 hectares and two active wineries. Its main white grape is the local Malvar.","terroir":"Plateau and hills at 700 to 800 m, limestone and clay.","producers":["Bodegas Mariscal"],"nerd_note":"Malvar, Mondéjar's white grape, is shared with the Madrid DO next door and is almost unknown elsewhere.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-monterrei":{"name":"Monterrei","cat":"DO","region":"Galicia","created":"1996","area_ha":795,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Doña Blanca, Godello, Treixadura; Mencía, Merenzao, Arauxa (Tempranillo)","summary":"Monterrei, around Verín on the Portuguese border, is Galicia's warmest and most continental DO, sitting in a valley shared with Portugal's Chaves. It makes fresh Godello, Treixadura and Doña Blanca whites and Mencía reds, with producers like Quinta da Muradella reviving old field blends. About 800 hectares.","terroir":"Valley and slopes at 350 to 900 m, with granite and slate soils and hot dry summers.","producers":["Quinta da Muradella","Pazos del Rey","Gargalo","Adegas Triay"],"nerd_note":"The fortress of Monterrei, above Verín, was the region's royal frontier castle; vineyards of Valle de Monterrei and Ladera de Monterrei are its two subzones.","contains":["Val de Monterrei","Ladeira de Monterrei"],"rslug":"galicia"},"es-montilla-moriles":{"name":"Montilla-Moriles","cat":"DO","region":"Andalusia","created":"1945","area_ha":4274,"styles":["fortified","sweet","white"],"grapes":"Pedro Ximénez (dominant), Airén (Lairén), Moscatel, Baladí, Torrontés, Verdejo, Chardonnay","summary":"Montilla-Moriles, south of Córdoba, makes the same family of solera-aged wines as Jerez, fino, amontillado, oloroso, palo cortado and PX, but from Pedro Ximénez grown in a hotter, inland climate. The difference matters: Pedro Ximénez here ripens to 15 percent or more naturally, so the finos and many other wines need no fortification at all. Montilla finos are softer, rounder and less salty than those of Jerez, and its sweet PX, from grapes dried in the sun on mats (*paseras*), are the benchmark of the style: Alvear, Toro Albalá and Pérez Barquero keep soleras over a century old.\n\nHistorically, Montilla's wines were sold to Jerez to sweeten or blend; the word *amontillado* means 'in the style of Montilla'. The DO, with about 4,300 hectares, also makes fermented young wines in earthenware *tinajas*, and its superior zones, Sierra de Montilla and Moriles Altos, have the best albero (albariza) soils.","terroir":"The Campiña of Córdoba, hot and continental (40 degrees in summer), with albariza soils (*albero*) in the best areas and sandy soils elsewhere.","producers":["Alvear","Toro Albalá","Pérez Barquero","Bodegas Robles","Bodegas Lagar Blanco","Bodegas Gracia","Navisa"],"nerd_note":"Amontillado is literally 'Montilla-style': it was the name Jerez gave to finos that had aged on into an amber, nutty style like the wines of Montilla.","contains":["Sierra de Montilla","Moriles Altos"],"rslug":"andalusia"},"es-montsant":{"name":"Montsant","cat":"DO","region":"Catalonia","created":"2001","area_ha":1761,"styles":["red","white","rose","sweet"],"grapes":"Garnatxa, Samsó (Cariñena), Ull de Llebre, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot; Garnatxa Blanca, Macabeu","summary":"Montsant wraps around Priorat like a horseshoe, and until 2001 it was the Falset subzone of DO Tarragona. It shares Priorat's Garnatxa and Samsó but on more varied soils: limestone, clay, granite, and only patches of slate. Wines are generally lighter and better value, and the region has many old vines. Leading names include Capçanes (whose cooperative makes Spain's best-known kosher wine), Acústic, Joan d'Anguera, Venus La Universal and Celler Laurona.","terroir":"Mostly 200 to 700 m on clay-limestone, granite, sand and some slate; Mediterranean climate.","producers":["Celler de Capçanes","Acústic Celler","Joan d'Anguera","Venus La Universal","Celler Laurona","Mas Perinet","Can Blau"],"nerd_note":"Falset's 1919 cooperative winery, by Cèsar Martinell, was one of the first 'cathedrals of wine' of Catalan modernisme.","rslug":"catalonia"},"es-navarra":{"name":"Navarra","cat":"DO","region":"Navarra","created":"1958","area_ha":8797,"styles":["rose","red","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Garnacha, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Graciano, Syrah; Chardonnay, Viura, Moscatel de Grano Menudo, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Navarra was once synonymous with rosado: bright, strawberry-scented Garnacha made by the *sangrado* (bleeding) method, a fixture of Spanish tables. In the 1980s, guided by the EVENA research station in Olite, it pivoted to Tempranillo, Cabernet and Merlot, producing approachable reds and blends. The modern revival is about old Garnacha from the Baja Montaña and high vineyards, from producers like Domaines Lupier, Viña Zorzal and Bodegas Máximo Abete, and about delicate *rosado*. Navarra also has a sweet tradition: Chivite's Colección 125 Vendimia Tardía Moscatel.","terroir":"Five subzones from cool, rainy Baja Montaña and Tierra Estella to the hot, dry Ribera Baja near Tudela, where the Bardenas Reales semi-desert begins. Soils are limestone, clay, and gravels.","producers":["Chivite","Ochoa","Domaines Lupier","Viña Zorzal","Bodegas Nekeas","Inurrieta","Otazu","Arínzano"],"nerd_note":"Navarra's rosados were traditionally made by bleeding juice off the vats, the *sangrado*; the colour comes from a few hours of skin contact.","contains":["Baja Montaña","Valdizarbe","Tierra Estella","Ribera Alta","Ribera Baja"],"rslug":"navarra"},"es-pago-de-arinzano":{"name":"Pago de Arínzano","cat":"VP","region":"Navarra","created":"2007","area_ha":127,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon; Chardonnay","summary":"An estate on a bend of the Ega river near Estella, with an 11th-century manor and a winery by Rafael Moneo, the first Vino de Pago outside Castilla-La Mancha. Developed by the Chivite family from 1988, it has since changed hands and is now sold as Señorío de Arínzano. Its microclimate combines Atlantic and Mediterranean influences.","terroir":"Limestone and alluvial soils on the Ega, at about 450 m.","producers":["Arínzano"],"nerd_note":"Its winery, a contemporary building by the Pritzker-winning architect Rafael Moneo, sits beside the restored medieval manor and chapel.","rslug":"navarra"},"es-pago-de-otazu":{"name":"Pago de Otazu","cat":"VP","region":"Navarra","created":"2008","area_ha":79,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Tempranillo; Chardonnay","summary":"The northernmost Vino de Pago in Spain, in the Etxauri valley near Pamplona, with a 19th-century winery, a medieval palace and a contemporary art collection. Its cool climate makes Chardonnay and Bordeaux-style reds.","terroir":"Gravel and clay soils in the cool valley of the Arga, where Atlantic influence is strong.","producers":["Bodega Otazu"],"nerd_note":"Otazu's barrel cellar doubles as a contemporary art gallery, and the estate has a 12th-century Romanesque church.","rslug":"navarra"},"es-pago-florentino":{"name":"Pago Florentino","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2009","area_ha":58,"styles":["red"],"grapes":"Tempranillo (Cencibel), Syrah, Petit Verdot","summary":"The Castilla-La Mancha pago of the Arzuaga family, best known for their Ribera del Duero winery, at Malagón in Ciudad Real, set among Mediterranean woodland at the foot of the Montes de Toledo. Its rules allow only Tempranillo, Syrah and Petit Verdot, and the estate makes a single red from stony soils at about 650 m.","terroir":"Stony soils at about 650 m.","producers":["Arzuaga Navarro"],"nerd_note":"Named after Florentino Arzuaga, founder of the family's Ribera winery.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-penedes":{"name":"Penedès","cat":"DO","region":"Penedès","area_ha":13500,"styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Xarel·lo, Macabeu, Parellada, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Malvasia de Sitges; Ull de Llebre, Garnatxa, Cabernet, Merlot, Sumoll","summary":"DO Penedès is the still and sparkling-wine DO of the region that is the heart of Cava. It has just over 13,000 registered hectares, of which only around 5,000 are used for Penedès wines; the rest goes to Cava. Since the 2025 harvest, all grapes and wines under the DO must be certified organic, a world first. Xarel·lo is the star: structured, herbal and ageworthy, as still wine or sparkling. *Clàssic Penedès*, the DO's own traditional-method sparkling category created in the early 2010s, requires organic grapes from Penedès and long lees ageing (at least 15 months).\n\nThe region also has a history of international varieties: Jean Leon planted Cabernet and Chardonnay in 1963 and Torres's Gran Coronas Black Label 1970 (today Mas La Plana) beat top Bordeaux at the 1979 Gault-Millau Wine Olympiad.","terroir":"From the Garraf coast to the Alt Penedès hills (up to about 800 m), limestone and clay soils; Mediterranean climate.","producers":["Familia Torres","Jean Leon","Can Ràfols dels Caus","Albet i Noya","Pardas","Gramona","Recaredo","Agustí Torelló Mata","Mas Candí"],"nerd_note":"Malvasia de Sitges, a sweet wine from the coastal town, is kept alive by the town's hospital, which has owned its historic vineyard since the 1930s.","rslug":"penedes"},"es-pla-de-bages":{"name":"Pla de Bages","cat":"DO","region":"Catalonia","styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Picapoll (local white), Macabeu, Chardonnay; Sumoll, Mandó, Ull de Llebre, Merlot, Cabernet, Garnatxa","summary":"Around Manresa, in the centre of Catalonia, Pla de Bages was a huge vineyard in the 19th century until phylloxera. Its symbols are the *tines*, round stone vats built in the vineyards to ferment wine on the spot, and Picapoll, a fresh, fragrant white. Abadal and Artium (a cooperative of Artés) lead the revival.","terroir":"Plateau at 400 to 600 m, cooler and continental, on sandstone and clay.","producers":["Abadal","Artium","Oller del Mas","Collbaix"],"nerd_note":"Hundreds of stone *tines* survive in the vineyards and woods of the Bages, some restored as heritage sites.","rslug":"catalonia"},"es-pla-i-llevant":{"name":"Pla i Llevant","cat":"DO","region":"Balearic Islands","created":"1999","area_ha":549,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Callet, Manto Negro, Fogoneu, Cabernet, Syrah, Merlot; Prensal Blanc, Parellada, Chardonnay","summary":"Pla i Llevant covers the plain (Pla) and eastern (Llevant) parts of Mallorca, around Felanitx, Manacor and Petra. Ànima Negra, founded in Felanitx in 1994, built its reputation on Callet, the island's dark, aromatic native red, and made Mallorca a name in wine.","terroir":"Calcareous red clay and limestone soils, Mediterranean climate.","producers":["Ànima Negra","Miquel Gelabert","Toni Gelabert","Vins Can Majoral"],"nerd_note":"Ànima Negra's AN and Son Negre are mostly Callet, a grape once dismissed as only good for blending.","rslug":"balearic-islands"},"es-prado-de-irache":{"name":"Prado de Irache","cat":"VP","region":"Navarra","created":"2008","area_ha":15,"styles":["red"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Garnacha, Graciano, Mazuela, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot","summary":"A small pago belonging to Bodegas Irache near Estella, next to the Monastery of Irache on the Camino de Santiago. At 15 hectares it is one of the smallest pagos in Spain.","terroir":"Limestone and clay at about 500 m.","producers":["Bodegas Irache"],"nerd_note":"Bodegas Irache's free wine fountain for pilgrims on the Camino has flowed since 1991.","rslug":"navarra"},"es-priorat":{"name":"Priorat / Priorato","cat":"DOQ","region":"Priorat","created":"1954 (DO); DOQ 2000, national 2009","area_ha":2229,"styles":["red","white","sweet","fortified"],"grapes":"Garnatxa, Samsó (Cariñena), Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot; Garnatxa Blanca, Macabeu, Pedro Ximénez","summary":"Priorat is Spain's most dramatic red-wine region and one of only two DOCa (in Catalan, DOQ) appellations. Its 2,200 hectares of vines grow on llicorella, a dark slate and quartzite that splits into flakes and forces roots deep for water; the yields from old Garnatxa and Samsó bush vines can fall below 1 kg per plant. The wines are powerful but, at their best, also fresh, with a mineral, graphite and dark-herb character and enormous ageing potential.\n\nThe modern legend began in 1989 with the 'Gang of Five' in Gratallops; Álvaro Palacios's L'Ermita, first made in 1993, became Spain's most expensive wine. Today some 115 wineries work here, from cooperatives that kept old vines alive to small producers like Terroir al Límit, Mas Doix, Ferrer Bobet, Clos Figueras and Mas Martinet. Since 2017 the region has a village and paratge system and its own Gran Vinya Classificada, Spain's equivalent of grand cru: L'Ermita, Mas de la Rosa and Tossal d'en Bou were listed in 2024.","terroir":"Llicorella slate and quartzite on steep slopes and terraces at 100 to 750 m, sheltered by the Montsant cliffs. A Mediterranean climate made continental by altitude, 400 to 600 mm of rain, and heat moderated by cool nights.","producers":["Álvaro Palacios","Clos Mogador","Clos Erasmus","Mas Martinet","Costers del Siurana (Clos de l'Obac)","Terroir al Límit","Mas Doix","Vall Llach","Ferrer Bobet","Cellers de Scala Dei","Clos Figueras","Mas d'en Gil"],"nerd_note":"Grape yields for Gran Vinya Classificada are capped at 3,000 kg/ha for reds, but the old vines of L'Ermita, on a steep north-east-facing slope of llicorella, give far less.","contains":["Gratallops","Porrera","Poboleda","Torroja del Priorat","Bellmunt del Priorat","el Lloar","la Morera de Montsant","Scala Dei","la Vilella Alta","la Vilella Baixa","Masos de Falset","Solanes del Molar"],"rslug":"priorat"},"es-rias-baixas":{"name":"Rías Baixas","cat":"DO","region":"Galicia","created":"1988","area_ha":4640,"styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Albariño (about 95%), Loureiro, Treixadura, Caíño Blanco, Torrontés, Godello; reds Caíño Tinto, Espadeiro, Sousón, Mencía, Brancellao, Loureiro Tinto","summary":"Rías Baixas is Spain's Atlantic white-wine capital, named after the drowned river valleys (*rías*) that cut into Galicia's southwest coast. Albariño, a thick-skinned, aromatic grape that stands up to rain, makes nearly all its wine: peach, citrus, white flowers and a salty, sea-air finish. The DO was founded in 1988 and now has about 4,600 hectares, divided into more than 20,000 tiny plots, and around 180 wineries. It sold almost 29 million litres in 2023/24, one of the five most valuable DOs in Spain.\n\nThe best wines are increasingly long-lived: lees-aged Albariño from Pazo de Señorans, Do Ferreiro, Zárate, Forjas del Salnés and Rodrigo Méndez can age for a decade or more, and old ungrafted vines survive in sandy coastal plots. The traditional trellis, the *parra*, is a pergola of granite posts and wires that lifts the canopy two metres above the ground for air.","terroir":"Five subzones: Val do Salnés (the coolest, wettest and the historic home of Albariño, around Cambados), Condado do Tea (warmer, inland along the Miño, with Treixadura and Loureiro in blends), O Rosal (at the Miño's mouth, with terraces and blends with Loureiro and Caíño), Soutomaior (a tiny zone near Pontevedra) and Ribeira do Ulla (in the north, inland from Padrón). Soils are mostly granite-based sandy loams, acidic and free-draining; rainfall is 1,600 mm on the coast.","producers":["Pazo de Señorans","Do Ferreiro (Gerardo Méndez)","Forjas del Salnés (Rodrigo Méndez)","Zárate","Palacio de Fefiñanes","Terras Gauda","Pazo Baión","Martín Códax","Granbazán","Bodegas La Val"],"nerd_note":"Albariño's Salnés stronghold has vines planted more than two centuries ago: Do Ferreiro's Cepas Vellas comes from a vineyard said to be over 200 years old, ungrafted on sandy soil.","contains":["Val do Salnés","Condado do Tea","O Rosal","Soutomaior","Ribeira do Ulla"],"rslug":"galicia"},"es-ribeira-sacra":{"name":"Ribeira Sacra","cat":"DO","region":"Galicia","created":"1996","area_ha":1310,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Mencía, Merenzao, Brancellao, Sousón, Caíño Tinto, Garnacha Tintorera; whites Godello, Albariño, Treixadura, Dona Branca","summary":"Ribeira Sacra, the 'sacred riverbank', takes its name from the Romanesque monasteries hidden in the forests of the Sil and Miño canyons. Its vineyards are among the most dramatic on earth: narrow stone terraces (*socalcos*) on slopes of up to 100 percent gradient, many of them first cut in Roman times, so steep that grapes are harvested by hand and hauled up in boxes on rails. This is *viticultura heroica*.\n\nThe red wines, mostly Mencía with Merenzao, Brancellao and Sousón, are light, perfumed and mineral, with a slate edge. The DO dates from 1996; it has about 1,300 hectares, nearly 100 wineries, and some of Spain's most talked-about producers, including Algueira, Guímaro, Dominio do Bibei, Envínate, Fazenda Prádio and Adega Vella.","terroir":"Five subzones: Amandi (the warmest, on the Sil's sunny north bank near Sober, with the steepest terraces), Chantada (the Miño around Belesar, cooler), Quiroga-Bibei (the upper Sil and Bibei, warmer, drier, with slate and Mediterranean scrub), Ribeiras do Miño and Ribeiras do Sil. Soils are slate, schist and granite; summer heat in the canyons ripens Mencía well despite Galicia's rain.","producers":["Adega Algueira","Guímaro","Dominio do Bibei","Envínate","Regina Viarum","Adega Vella","Fazenda Prádio","Ponte da Boga"],"nerd_note":"Ribeira Sacra has been pursuing UNESCO World Heritage status as a cultural landscape of Roman and monastic terraces; local legend says Amandi's wine was sent to Rome for the emperors.","contains":["Amandi","Chantada","Quiroga-Bibei","Ribeiras do Miño","Ribeiras do Sil"],"rslug":"galicia"},"es-ribeiro":{"name":"Ribeiro","cat":"DO","region":"Galicia","created":"1932 (Estatuto)","area_ha":1270,"styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Treixadura, Torrontés, Godello, Loureiro, Albariño, Lado, Caíño Blanco; reds Caíño, Sousón, Brancellao, Ferrón, Mencía","summary":"Ribeiro, in the valleys of the Miño, Avia and Arnoia around Ribadavia, was Galicia's great medieval wine export, shipped to England in the 14th century and celebrated in the Golden Age. Today it is a small DO (about 1,270 hectares) and a place of quiet revival: Treixadura blends with Albariño, Loureiro and Godello make fresh, fragrant whites, and producers such as Emilio Rojo, Coto de Gomariz, Luis Anxo Rodríguez and Viña Meín are bringing back old vineyards and rare grapes. Ribeiro also makes *tostado*, a sweet wine of grapes dried on straw mats.","terroir":"Terraces and slopes above three rivers at 75 to 400 m, mostly granite with sand, warmer and drier than the coast.","producers":["Viña Meín","Emilio Rojo","Coto de Gomariz","Luis Anxo Rodríguez (Arsenio Paz)","Casal de Armán"],"nerd_note":"Ribeiro's medieval wine trade was controlled from Ribadavia's Jewish quarter, and the town still holds a Festa da Istoria commemorating it.","rslug":"galicia"},"es-ribera-del-duero":{"name":"Ribera del Duero","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla y León","created":"1982","area_ha":27323,"styles":["red","rose","white"],"grapes":"Tempranillo (Tinto Fino, Tinta del País), with Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Malbec, Garnacha; Albillo Mayor for whites (at least 75%)","summary":"Ribera del Duero is the other half of Spain's red-wine aristocracy. It follows the Duero for about 115 km through four provinces (Burgos, Valladolid, Soria and Segovia), from San Esteban de Gormaz in the east to Quintanilla de Onésimo in the west, where the river runs past Vega Sicilia, Pingus, Aalto and Abadía Retuerta in a stretch nicknamed the Golden Mile. The DO dates only from 1982, when a few producers, Alejandro Fernández of Pesquera prominent among them, persuaded Madrid that the region was more than a source of rosado. Vega Sicilia, founded in 1864, had already been making legendary wine here for a century.\n\nThe wines are built on Tinto Fino, a thick-skinned local strain of Tempranillo that ripens in a short, hot season at 750 to 950 m, with nights cold enough to frost the valleys in May and September. The result is dark, firm, concentrated red, with black fruit, liquorice and minerals, often aged in French oak; the best age for decades. The DO now has about 27,300 registered hectares and roughly 250 to 300 wineries, from cooperatives to Pingus's few thousand bottles. Since the 2019 reform, white wines made mainly from Albillo Mayor are allowed, and old-style claretes, part white grapes, are being revived. Most of the vineyard lies in the province of Burgos, around Aranda, La Horra, Roa and Gumiel.","terroir":"A high plateau 720 to 1,000 m up, with continental extremes, about 450 mm of rain, very cold winters and big day-night swings. Soils range from alluvial sands and gravels near the river to limestone and marl on the slopes and the chalky páramo tablelands above; the eastern Soria sector is highest and coldest.","producers":["Vega Sicilia","Dominio de Pingus","Tinto Pesquera","Aalto","Hacienda Monasterio","Pago de Carraovejas","Emilio Moro","Protos","Alión","Arzuaga","Dominio del Águila","Bodegas Rodero"],"nerd_note":"Vega Sicilia's Único is released only after about a decade of ageing in vats, barrels and bottle, and some vintages wait far longer: the 1968 was released only in 1991, after 23 years.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-ribera-del-guadiana":{"name":"Ribera del Guadiana","cat":"DO","region":"Extremadura","created":"1999","area_ha":40976,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Garnacha, Cabernet, Syrah, Merlot; Pardina, Cayetana Blanca, Macabeo, Eva (Beba), Chardonnay","summary":"Extremadura's only DO, with about 41,000 hectares in six subzones: Tierra de Barros (the largest, around Almendralejo), Matanegra, Ribera Alta, Ribera Baja, Montánchez and Cañamero. Mostly a source of value wine, it has a few ambitious producers.","terroir":"Plains of clay (*barros*) and sand, and hilly areas in Cañamero and Montánchez.","producers":["Bodegas Habla","Palacio Quemado (Alvear)","Bodegas Toribio","Viña Santa Marina"],"nerd_note":"Montánchez has a tradition of *pitarra*, young wine made in earthenware jars.","contains":["Tierra de Barros","Matanegra","Ribera Alta","Ribera Baja","Montánchez","Cañamero"],"rslug":"extremadura"},"es-ribera-del-jucar":{"name":"Ribera del Júcar","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2003","area_ha":6500,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Bobal, Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot; Moscatel, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Ribera del Júcar covers seven municipalities in southern Cuenca along the Júcar river, with about 6,500 registered hectares but only nine bottling wineries in 2023/24, so most of its grapes go to cooperatives and bulk wine. Its bottled reds rely on Bobal and Tempranillo grown on stony limestone at about 700 m.","terroir":"Plateau at about 700 m with stony limestone soils.","producers":["Bodegas Illana"],"nerd_note":"One of the smallest numbers of active wineries for its size in Spain, with only nine registered in 2023/24.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-rio-negro":{"name":"Río Negro","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2025","area_ha":470,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Tinto Fragoso, Syrah, Cabernet, Merlot; Gewürztraminer","summary":"Registered by the EU in March 2025, Río Negro covers just over 470 hectares in Cogolludo, San Andrés and Membrillera in Guadalajara, with the only winery in the delimited area. Its rules list Tinto Fragoso, a native red variety, and a white from 100 percent Gewürztraminer.","terroir":"Hills in northern Guadalajara, toward the Sierra de Ayllón.","nerd_note":"It was the 105th Spanish DOP and the 24th in Castilla-La Mancha.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-rioja":{"name":"Rioja","cat":"DOCa","region":"Rioja","created":"1925 (Consejo; DO 1932 statute; DOCa 1991)","area_ha":66499,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Tempranillo (about 88% of reds), Garnacha, Mazuelo, Graciano, Maturana Tinta; whites Viura, Malvasía Riojana, Garnacha Blanca, Tempranillo Blanco, Maturana Blanca, Turruntés, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Verdejo","summary":"Rioja is the benchmark Spanish red and, with 66,500 registered hectares and around 2.4 million hectolitres sold a year, also one of Europe's largest fine-wine regions. The classic Rioja is a blend led by Tempranillo, aged in 225-litre barrels, traditionally American oak, until it turns soft and savoury: strawberry and dried cherry, vanilla and dill, leather and tobacco. That style was learned from Bordeaux in the 1850s to 1880s and is still made beautifully by López de Heredia, La Rioja Alta, CVNE and Muga, whose Gran Reservas can live for half a century.\n\nFrom the 1990s, a modern wave led by Artadi, Roda, Remírez de Ganuza, Finca Allende and Benjamín Romeo made darker, riper wines in new French oak, sold on the name of a vineyard rather than an ageing category. Today the most interesting movement is about place: Telmo Rodríguez and Remelluri, Sierra Cantabria, Olivier Rivière, Abel Mendoza and many small growers in San Vicente de la Sonsierra and Rioja Alavesa make transparent wines from old field blends, often from bush vines high on the slopes. The rules have followed: since 2017 a label can name one of three zones, a village (*vino de pueblo*) or one of 167 *viñedos singulares*.\n\nRioja's whites deserve more attention. Barrel-aged Viura and Malvasía from López de Heredia (Viña Tondonia Blanco) and Murrieta (Castillo Ygay Blanco) are among the world's most singular long-lived white wines, and white plantings have doubled in twenty years.","terroir":"The DOCa spans the Ebro valley from the Conchas de Haro to Alfaro, protected from the Atlantic by the Sierra de Cantabria and fed by seven tributaries from the south. Rioja Alta (cool, clay-limestone and ferrous clay, alluvial terraces), Rioja Alavesa (chalky clay-limestone terraces under the Sierra de Cantabria) and Rioja Oriental (warmer, drier, alluvial soils, Garnacha country) give three distinct climates, and altitude ranges from under 300 m to over 700 m.","producers":["R. López de Heredia","La Rioja Alta","CVNE","Muga","Marqués de Murrieta","Marqués de Riscal","Bodegas Roda","Artadi","Remírez de Ganuza","Contino","Remelluri","Sierra Cantabria","Palacios Remondo","Finca Allende","Bodegas Bilbaínas","Gómez Cruzado"],"nerd_note":"Rioja's minimum ageing for a red Gran Reserva (two years in oak and two in bottle within five) is stricter than the national rule, but the real eccentric is López de Heredia, which still ages its Viña Tondonia whites for years in old barrels in cellars dug in 1877 and makes its own casks with an in-house cooper.","contains":["Rioja Alta","Rioja Alavesa","Rioja Oriental"],"rslug":"rioja"},"es-rosalejo":{"name":"Rosalejo","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2024","area_ha":1339,"styles":["red"],"grapes":"Varieties not documented in the sources consulted","summary":"Registered in March 2024, Rosalejo is one of Spain's newest pagos. The Ministry of Agriculture's 2023/24 report lists it with 1,338.7 hectares of land and one winery, but no wine marketed during its first campaign. Grape varieties and producer details were not available in the sources consulted.","terroir":"Castilla-La Mancha.","nerd_note":"The ministry recorded no wine marketed under Rosalejo in its first campaign.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-rueda":{"name":"Rueda","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla y León","created":"1980","area_ha":20735,"styles":["white","sparkling","red","fortified"],"grapes":"Verdejo (dominant), Sauvignon Blanc, Viura, Palomino; reds from Tempranillo since 2008","summary":"Rueda, south of Valladolid, is Spain's best-selling white wine DO, and its fortunes rest on one grape: Verdejo. It used to make oxidative, sherry-like whites, *dorado* and *pálido*, aged in glass demijohns in the sun. In 1972 Marqués de Riscal, advised by Émile Peynaud, came looking for white grapes and began making fresh, cool-fermented Verdejo; the DO followed in 1980. Today Rueda has about 20,700 hectares and sells almost 900,000 hectolitres a year. The best wines, from old bush vines on stony soils around La Seca, Serrada and Rueda, can be textured and long-lived, especially when aged on lees or in barrel: Belondrade, Ossian and José Pariente show the range, and a newer *Gran Vino de Rueda* category rewards old vines and low yields.","terroir":"A flat plateau at 700 to 850 m, continental, with sandy and stony alluvial soils (cascajo) rich in limestone, which give Verdejo freshness despite the heat.","producers":["Marqués de Riscal","Belondrade","José Pariente","Ossian","Bodegas Naia","Javier Sanz","Menade"],"nerd_note":"Rueda Dorado, an oxidative fortified white aged for at least two years in glass and wood, is still defined by the DO: a living fossil of the region's medieval style.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-sierra-de-salamanca":{"name":"Sierra de Salamanca","cat":"VC","region":"Castilla y León","created":"2010","area_ha":128,"styles":["red","rose","white"],"grapes":"Rufete (local), Tempranillo, Garnacha; Viura, Moscatel","summary":"In the Sierra de Francia, south of Salamanca, terraced vineyards of granite and slate around Mogarraz and San Esteban de la Sierra grow Rufete, a fragile, fragrant local red that makes light, Pinot-like wines. About 130 hectares.","terroir":"Mountain terraces at 400 to 1,000 m with a wetter Atlantic-influenced climate.","producers":["Bodega La Zorra","Cámbrico"],"nerd_note":"Rufete is also grown in Portugal's Dão and Douro as Tinta Pinheira.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-sierras-de-malaga":{"name":"Sierras de Málaga","cat":"DO","region":"Andalusia","created":"2001","area_ha":440,"styles":["white","red","rose"],"grapes":"Moscatel, Pedro Ximénez, Chardonnay, Doradilla; Tempranillo, Syrah, Merlot, Cabernet, Petit Verdot, Romé","summary":"Sierras de Málaga covers dry wines from the province's mountains, with the subzone Serranía de Ronda as its star. Around Ronda, at 600 to 1,000 m, a wave of estates since the 1990s, led by the German Friedrich Schatz, Descalzos Viejos, Cortijo Los Aguilares and Bodega Joaquín Fernández, has shown that the mountains can make fresh reds from Pinot Noir, Petit Verdot and local Romé. In the Axarquía, Telmo Rodríguez and Jorge Ordóñez make dry Moscatel.","terroir":"Mountain slopes of slate, clay and limestone; Ronda's continental-Mediterranean climate has cold winters and cool nights.","producers":["F. Schatz","Descalzos Viejos","Cortijo Los Aguilares","Bodega Joaquín Fernández","Bodegas Bentomiz","Telmo Rodríguez"],"nerd_note":"Descalzos Viejos's winery is built in a 16th-century Trinitarian convent below Ronda's famous gorge.","contains":["Serranía de Ronda"],"rslug":"andalusia"},"es-somontano":{"name":"Somontano","cat":"DO","region":"Aragón","created":"1984","area_ha":3724,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Tempranillo, Syrah, Garnacha, Moristel, Parraleta; Chardonnay, Gewürztraminer, Macabeo","summary":"Somontano, 'below the mountain', sits at the foot of the Pyrenees around Barbastro in Huesca. It is cooler than the rest of Aragón and became the poster child for international varieties in the 1990s: Chardonnay, Gewürztraminer, Cabernet. Local grapes Moristel and Parraleta are coming back. Enate, Viñas del Vero and Blecua are the leading names.","terroir":"Foothills at 350 to 650 m, with limestone and clay soils and a continental climate cooled by mountain air.","producers":["Enate","Viñas del Vero","Blecua","Pirineos","Lalanne"],"nerd_note":"Enate commissions artists like Antoni Tàpies and Eduardo Chillida for its labels and has a contemporary art collection.","rslug":"arag-n"},"es-tacoronte-acentejo":{"name":"Tacoronte-Acentejo","cat":"DO","region":"Canary Islands","created":"1992","area_ha":695,"styles":["red","white","rose","sweet"],"grapes":"Listán Negro, Negramoll, Listán Blanco, Malvasía, Vijariego, Castellana Negra","summary":"The first Canary Island DO (1992), on the northeast slopes of Tenerife between Tacoronte and La Matanza, the most planted area of the island. Its reds from Listán Negro and Negramoll, often made by carbonic maceration, are juicy and peppery. The Casa del Vino de Tenerife, in El Sauzal, is the island's wine museum.","terroir":"North-facing volcanic slopes at 100 to 1,000 m, with trade-wind clouds and relatively high rainfall.","producers":["Bodegas Monje","Bodega Insulares Tenerife","Presas Ocampo","Viñátigo"],"nerd_note":"Tacoronte-Acentejo was the first Canary DO, recognised in 1992, a few years before the rest of the islands followed.","rslug":"canary-islands"},"es-tarragona":{"name":"Tarragona","cat":"DO","region":"Catalonia","area_ha":2640,"styles":["red","white","rose","fortified","sweet"],"grapes":"Garnatxa, Samsó, Ull de Llebre, Cabernet, Merlot, Syrah; Macabeu, Xarel·lo, Parellada, Garnatxa Blanca, Moscatel","summary":"One of Spain's historic DOs, around the Roman capital Tarraco, Tarragona once made sweet altar wine (*vi de missa*) and long-aged fortified *Tarragona Clásico*. Today it is mostly a source of everyday reds and whites in two subzones, Camp de Tarragona and Ribera d'Ebre. Reus was the hub of the 19th-century wine and brandy trade.","terroir":"Coastal plain and hills (Camp de Tarragona) and the warmer Ebro valley (Ribera d'Ebre), on limestone and alluvial soils.","producers":["De Muller","Vinícola de Nulles"],"nerd_note":"De Muller, founded in 1851, was a supplier of altar wine to the Vatican and still keeps soleras of rancio and sweet wines started in the 19th century.","contains":["Camp de Tarragona","Ribera d'Ebre"],"rslug":"catalonia"},"es-terra-alta":{"name":"Terra Alta","cat":"DO","region":"Catalonia","created":"1982","area_ha":5670,"styles":["white","red","rose","sweet","fortified"],"grapes":"Garnatxa Blanca (signature), Macabeu, Parellada; Garnatxa, Samsó, Garnatxa Peluda, Syrah","summary":"Terra Alta, the high land in the far southwest of Catalonia, is the world's great stronghold of Garnatxa Blanca, which here gives broad, herbal, saline whites. The DO recognises a *Terra Alta Garnatxa Blanca* label for wines made only from it. Reds of Garnatxa and Garnatxa Peluda (a hairy-leaved form) are also good. The region was a Civil War battlefield in 1938 (the Battle of the Ebro), and its modernist cooperative wineries in Gandesa and El Pinell de Brai are monuments.","terroir":"Plateau and hills at 350 to 600 m, with chalky clay soils, a continental-Mediterranean climate, and two winds, the dry *cerç* from the north and the humid *garbinada* from the sea.","producers":["Edetària","Celler Piñol","Herència Altés","Celler Frisach","Bàrbara Forés","Lafou"],"nerd_note":"Terra Alta's cooperative at El Pinell de Brai has a ceramic frieze about wine and agriculture by Xavier Nogués on its modernist facade.","rslug":"catalonia"},"es-tharsys":{"name":"Tharsys","cat":"VP","region":"Valencia","created":"2025","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Bobal and other reds; whites and sparkling","summary":"Spain's newest Vino de Pago (EU, October 2025), near Requena. The historic estate was revived in 1998 under its original name, and its pago rules cover quality sparkling wine as well as still red, white and rosé.","terroir":"Requena plateau, limestone and clay.","producers":["Pago de Tharsys"],"nerd_note":"With Tharsys, Spain counted 28 Vinos de Pago.","rslug":"valencia"},"es-tierra-del-vino-de-zamora":{"name":"Tierra del Vino de Zamora","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla y León","created":"2007","area_ha":574,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Garnacha, Cabernet Sauvignon; Malvasía, Moscatel, Albillo, Verdejo, Palomino","summary":"South of Zamora, between Toro and Salamanca, the historically named 'Land of Wine' has old bush vines on sandy and clay soils, many ungrafted. About 570 hectares and a handful of wineries, with reds of Tempranillo less heavy than Toro's.","terroir":"Plateau at 650 to 800 m, continental, clay and sand soils.","producers":["Viñas del Cénit","Bodegas El Soto"],"nerd_note":"The district has been called Tierra del Vino since the Middle Ages, a name that predates any wine law by centuries.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-toro":{"name":"Toro","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla y León","created":"1987","area_ha":5437,"styles":["red","rose","white"],"grapes":"Tinta de Toro (a local Tempranillo), Garnacha; Malvasía and Verdejo for whites","summary":"Toro, in the province of Zamora, makes some of Spain's most powerful reds from Tinta de Toro, a thick-skinned local form of Tempranillo grown on sandy, stony soils. The wines had a reputation in medieval times, reportedly carried on Columbus's ships, but the DO dates only from 1987. In the late 1990s Vega Sicilia (Pintia), the Eguren family (Numanthia, Teso La Monja), Mariano García and the Lurton brothers arrived, attracted by ungrafted old vines and the possibility of making big, dark wines. The challenge in Toro today is freshness: earlier picking and less oak are making the wines more elegant.","terroir":"A dry plateau at 600 to 750 m with about 350 to 400 mm of rain and more than 2,600 hours of sun. Sandy soils over clay and limestone, with river pebbles, allowed many ungrafted pre-phylloxera vines to survive.","producers":["Numanthia","Pintia (Vega Sicilia)","Teso La Monja","Bodegas Fariña","Elías Mora","San Román","Quinta de la Quietud"],"nerd_note":"Toro's sandy soils protected vines from phylloxera, and its old ungrafted Tinta de Toro is a reason why foreign estates came; Teso La Monja's Alma is from ungrafted vines over 100 years old.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-ucles":{"name":"Uclés","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2005","area_ha":1700,"styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Garnacha; Verdejo, Moscatel, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Uclés, in northwest Cuenca and eastern Toledo around the Monastery of Uclés, the 'El Escorial of La Mancha', is a small DO in the hills of the Sierra de Altomira, cooler than the plain.","terroir":"Hills at 600 to 1,200 m with limestone and clay.","producers":["Bodegas Fontana","Finca La Estacada"],"nerd_note":"Uclés's huge monastery was the headquarters of the Order of Santiago from the 12th century.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-urbezo":{"name":"Urbezo","cat":"VP","region":"Aragón","created":"2024","area_ha":230,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Garnacha, Cabernet Sauvignon, Mazuela, Merlot, Syrah, Tempranillo; Chardonnay, Garnacha Blanca, Moscatel de Alejandría","summary":"Registered by the EU in October 2024 as Spain's 26th Vino de Pago and Aragón's second, Urbezo covers just over 230 hectares in Cariñena on the slopes of the Sierra de Algairén at about 490 m. The application was filed in 2019 by the estate's winery (Solar de Urbezo).","terroir":"Foothills of the Sierra de Algairén, slate and clay soils.","producers":["Solar de Urbezo"],"nerd_note":"Spain's ministry list files Urbezo under the DO term rather than VP, but the ministry's own announcement calls it a vino de pago.","rslug":"arag-n"},"es-uruena":{"name":"Urueña","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla y León","created":"2021","area_ha":16,"styles":["red","rose"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah","summary":"A Vino de Pago just north of the walled hilltop town of Urueña in western Valladolid, made by Heredad de Urueña, the only producer. Registered by the EU in November 2021.","terroir":"Limestone plateau at about 800 m.","producers":["Heredad de Urueña"],"nerd_note":"Urueña is also Spain's 'Villa del Libro', a town of bookshops inside medieval walls.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-utiel-requena":{"name":"Utiel-Requena","cat":"DO","region":"Valencia","created":"1957","area_ha":32036,"styles":["red","rose","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Bobal (the majority), Tempranillo, Garnacha, Cabernet, Syrah; Macabeo, Tardana, Chardonnay","summary":"Utiel-Requena, a high plateau 70 km inland from Valencia, is the stronghold of Bobal. Long used for bulk wine and colour, Bobal is now made into fresh, dark, juicy reds and fine rosados from old bush vines. The region has about 32,000 hectares, five Vinos de Pago (El Terrerazo, Los Balagueses, Chozas Carrascal, Vera de Estenas and Tharsys) and the Levante zone of Cava. Mustiguillo's Toni Sarrión led the Bobal revival in the late 1990s.","terroir":"Plateau at 600 to 900 m, continental, with limestone and clay soils and frosts.","producers":["Mustiguillo","Bodegas Hispano-Suizas","Chozas Carrascal","Bruno Murciano","Bodega Sierra Norte","Vera de Estenas"],"nerd_note":"Bobal's name is usually traced to Latin *bovale*, for the bull's-head shape of its bunches.","rslug":"valencia"},"es-valdeorras":{"name":"Valdeorras","cat":"DO","region":"Galicia","created":"1945 (Consejo)","area_ha":1200,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Godello (signature), Doña Blanca, Palomino; Mencía, Merenzao, Sousón, Garnacha Tintorera","summary":"Valdeorras, the 'valley of gold' on the upper Sil in eastern Ourense, is the birthplace of the Godello revival. In the 1970s a group of local growers, Horacio Fernández among them, propagated Godello from a few surviving vines in a recovery project known as REVIVAL, and today it is one of Spain's finest white grapes: textured, citrus, saline, ageworthy. Rafael Palacios (brother of Álvaro) moved to O Bolo in 2004 and his As Sortes and O Soro are benchmarks; Telmo Rodríguez's Gaba do Xil and Valdesil also show the region.","terroir":"Slate and granite slopes at 300 to 700 m, with a mixed Atlantic and continental climate; the higher, eastern sector (O Bolo, Larouco) is particularly cool.","producers":["Rafael Palacios","Valdesil","Godeval","Telmo Rodríguez (Gaba do Xil)","Bodegas Avancia","A Coroa"],"nerd_note":"The Romans mined gold in the Sil, hence the valley's name; Godeval's winery in O Barco is housed in the 12th-century Xagoaza monastery.","rslug":"galicia"},"es-valdepenas":{"name":"Valdepeñas","cat":"DO","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"1932","area_ha":12171,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Tempranillo (Cencibel), Garnacha, Cabernet Sauvignon; Airén, Macabeo","summary":"Valdepeñas, an enclave inside La Mancha in the south of Ciudad Real, has been Madrid's tavern wine for centuries, aged in *tinajas* and later in American oak. The DO, one of the first named in 1932, has about 12,000 hectares but only a dozen bottlers, among them Félix Solís, one of Spain's largest wine companies. Its soft, oaky Cencibel reservas and gran reservas are among the best values in Spanish wine.","terroir":"Plateau at about 700 m, with red clay over limestone and very hot summers.","producers":["Félix Solís","Bodegas Navarro López","Bodegas Real","Los Llanos"],"nerd_note":"Valdepeñas's name means 'valley of stones', and its old wineries still hold rows of earthenware tinajas taller than a person.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-valencia":{"name":"Valencia","cat":"DO","region":"Valencia","created":"1957","area_ha":7644,"styles":["white","red","rose","sweet","fortified","sparkling"],"grapes":"Moscatel de Alejandría, Merseguera, Macabeo, Malvasía, Pedro Ximénez; Monastrell, Garnacha Tintorera, Bobal, Tempranillo","summary":"DO Valencia has four subzones: Alto Turia (high, cool whites), Valentino (central hills), Moscatel de Valencia (sweet Moscatel, often fortified, near the coast) and Clariano (south, reds). A lively group of producers including Celler del Roure (which ferments in old buried tinajas) and Rafael Cambra are reviving Mandó and Monastrell.","terroir":"From coastal plain to interior hills up to 1,000 m; limestone soils.","producers":["Celler del Roure","Rafael Cambra","Bodegas Murviedro","Vicente Gandía"],"nerd_note":"Moscatel de Valencia is a *vino de licor*, often made by adding alcohol to fresh must, a style called *mistela*.","contains":["Alto Turia","Valentino","Moscatel de Valencia","Clariano"],"rslug":"valencia"},"es-valle-de-guimar":{"name":"Valle de Güímar","cat":"DO","region":"Canary Islands","created":"1990s","area_ha":160,"styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Listán Blanco, Malvasía, Moscatel, Gual; Listán Negro","summary":"On the east coast of Tenerife, south of Santa Cruz, vines rise to about 1,500 m on volcanic soils, some of the highest vineyards in Europe. Listán Blanco makes most of the wine.","terroir":"East-facing slopes from 200 to 1,500 m, sheltered from trade winds, with pumice and ash soils.","producers":["Bodegas Comarcal Valle de Güímar"],"nerd_note":"Some of Güímar's vineyards, at up to 1,500 m, are among the highest in Europe.","rslug":"canary-islands"},"es-valle-de-la-orotava":{"name":"Valle de la Orotava","cat":"DO","region":"Canary Islands","created":"1990s","area_ha":170,"styles":["red","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Listán Negro, Listán Blanco, Negramoll, Malvasía, Tintilla, Baboso, Vijariego","summary":"The Orotava valley, below Mount Teide on Tenerife's north coast, is home to a unique vine-training system, the *cordón trenzado* (braided cordon): long canes of old ungrafted Listán vines are braided together into horizontal ropes up to 10 metres long, propped on sticks, and a new cane is added each year. Suertes del Marqués, Envínate and Tajinaste have made the valley's mineral, smoky Listán Negro and Listán Blanco famous.","terroir":"North-facing slopes from 250 to 800 m, volcanic soils of ash and lava, trade-wind clouds.","producers":["Suertes del Marqués","Envínate","Tajinaste","Bodegas El Penitente","Bodega Ignios Orígenes"],"nerd_note":"Because each year's new cane is braided into the old cordon, a cordón trenzado vine is a living record of its own growth, and many are well over a century old.","rslug":"canary-islands"},"es-vallegarcia":{"name":"Vallegarcía","cat":"VP","region":"Castilla-La Mancha","created":"2019","area_ha":50,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot; Viognier","summary":"A pago at Retuerta del Bullaque in the Montes de Toledo (Ciudad Real), on a large estate at about 850 m that shares its municipality with Dehesa del Carrizal. It made its name with Syrah and with Viognier, one of the first planted in Spain, and Bordeaux-style reds.","terroir":"Slate and clay at about 850 m.","producers":["Pago de Vallegarcía"],"nerd_note":"Its Viognier was among the first in Spain.","rslug":"castilla-la-mancha"},"es-valles-de-benavente":{"name":"Valles de Benavente","cat":"VC","region":"Castilla y León","created":"2000s","area_ha":146,"styles":["red","rose","white"],"grapes":"Prieto Picudo, Tempranillo, Mencía; Verdejo, Moscatel","summary":"A Vino de Calidad around the town of Benavente in northern Zamora, where the Esla, Órbigo and Tera rivers converge. Small (about 146 hectares) and focused on Prieto Picudo and Tempranillo.","terroir":"Alluvial valleys at 700 to 800 m.","nerd_note":"One of four Vinos de Calidad in Castilla y León, a category the 2003 law conceived as a step toward full DO status.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-valtiendas":{"name":"Valtiendas","cat":"VC","region":"Castilla y León","created":"2009 (EU)","area_ha":72,"styles":["red","rose"],"grapes":"Tempranillo (Tinta del País), Garnacha, Syrah","summary":"A small Vino de Calidad in northern Segovia, just south of Ribera del Duero along the Duratón, with vineyards up to 900 m. Only about 70 hectares and a few producers.","terroir":"High, cold limestone plateau.","nerd_note":"Its villages sit along the Duratón gorge, a natural park famous for griffon vultures.","rslug":"castilla-y-le-n"},"es-vera-de-estenas":{"name":"Vera de Estenas","cat":"VP","region":"Valencia","created":"2019","area_ha":45,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Bobal, Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot; Chardonnay","summary":"The Martínez Bermell family estate at Utiel, one of the first in the area to bottle Bobal-based wines, registered as a pago by the EU in 2019. Its clay and limestone soils at about 750 m give Bobal and Tempranillo reds and a Chardonnay; with El Terrerazo, it shows how Utiel's plateau has turned from bulk wine to estate wine.","terroir":"Clay and limestone at about 750 m.","producers":["Vera de Estenas"],"nerd_note":"Vera de Estenas is a member of the Unión de Vinos de Pago.","rslug":"valencia"},"es-vinos-de-madrid":{"name":"Vinos de Madrid","cat":"DO","region":"Madrid","created":"1990","area_ha":5898,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Garnacha, Tempranillo, Cabernet, Syrah; Malvar, Albillo Real, Airén","summary":"Vinos de Madrid covers about 5,900 hectares in four subzones. Arganda (southeast, Tempranillo and Malvar), Navalcarnero (southwest) and El Molar (north, added in 2019) make everyday wine; San Martín de Valdeiglesias, in the foothills of the Sierra de Gredos, makes the stars. There, Comando G (Daniel Jiménez-Landi and Fernando García, with Marc Isart at the start), Bernabeleva, Marañones and 4 Monos make translucent, floral Garnacha from old bush vines on granite, sometimes compared to Burgundy or Châteauneuf.","terroir":"From clay plains near Madrid to granite slopes up to 900 m in the west.","producers":["Comando G","Bernabeleva","Bodega Marañones","4 Monos","Bodegas Licinia","Jesús Díaz"],"nerd_note":"Comando G's Rumbo al Norte comes from a tiny, north-facing parcel high in the Gredos, one of the highest Garnacha sites in Spain.","contains":["Arganda","Navalcarnero","San Martín de Valdeiglesias","El Molar"],"rslug":"madrid"},"es-ycoden-daute-isora":{"name":"Ycoden-Daute-Isora","cat":"DO","region":"Canary Islands","created":"1990s","area_ha":79,"styles":["white","red","rose","sweet"],"grapes":"Listán Blanco, Malvasía, Vijariego, Marmajuelo; Listán Negro, Negramoll, Tintilla","summary":"On Tenerife's northwest, from Icod de los Vinos and Garachico to Guía de Isora, this DO was the heart of the 16th and 17th century Canary sack trade. Garachico's harbour, from which wine was exported to England, was buried by lava in 1706. Today it makes fresh whites from Listán Blanco and Malvasía.","terroir":"Volcanic slopes from sea level to 1,400 m.","producers":["Bodegas Viñátigo","Bodegas Tierra de Frontos"],"nerd_note":"Icod de los Vinos takes its name from wine and is home to the famous Drago Milenario dragon tree.","rslug":"canary-islands"},"es-yecla":{"name":"Yecla","cat":"DO","region":"Murcia","created":"1975","area_ha":4103,"styles":["red","rose","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Monastrell, Garnacha Tintorera, Syrah, Tempranillo, Merlot, Cabernet; Airén, Macabeo, Merseguera","summary":"Yecla is one of the few Spanish DOs to cover a single municipality. Its Monastrell, grown on limestone at 400 to 800 m, is led by Bodegas Castaño, whose family helped bring the region from bulk wine to bottled exports.","terroir":"Plateau at 400 to 800 m with limestone soils and semi-arid climate.","producers":["Bodegas Castaño","Bodegas Barahonda","La Purísima"],"nerd_note":"Yecla has two subzones, Campo Arriba (higher, old vines) and Campo Abajo.","rslug":"murcia"}}};
